2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BADEN.htm#OttoIEbersteindied1279
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.        ''' OTTO [I] von Eberstein '''(-1279, bur Herrenalb).  "Eberhardus…dominus de Eberstein" confirmed a donation to Kloster Herrenalb by charter dated 1207, witnessed by "Eberhardo et Ottone domicellis de Eberstein, Alberto
2 CONC  de Rastede et Eberhardo fratre suo…"[766].  "O[tto] de Eberstein et frater meus Eberhardus" agreed a partition of their territories by charter dated 1219, witnessed by "Bertholdo et Aluico comitibus de Sultze, Gotfrido comiti de 
2 CONC Calwe…"[767].  Otto´s territories included property in Kraichgau.  The Annales Wormatienses name "domni Eberhardi de Eberstein et domni E. filii sui, comitis de Sein et domni Ottonis de Eberstein" in 1249[768].  “Otto junior domin
2 CONC us de Eberstein” confirmed the marriage between “Adelheidim...filiam meam” and “Henrico nobili viro juniori de Lichtenberc”, appointing as fiduciaries “Eberhardem seniorem de Eberstein, prepositum Argentinensem fratres meos...”, b
2 CONC y charter dated 8 Jan 1251[769].  "Otto de Eberstein cum domina Beatrice nostri…thori socia, domini Wolfradi nobilis de Crutheim filia" renounced rights in the inheritance of "domini Cunradi…patrui nostri senioris de Cruthein" by 
2 CONC charter dated 22 Jul 1252, witnessed by "dominum Kraftonem de Bockesberc, dominum Gotefridum de Hohinloch nobiles…"[770].  He is consistently, although not exclusively, referred to in primary sources as Graf von Eberstein from [12
2 CONC 60][771].  "Graf Otto von Eberstein…und seine Söhne Otto, Wolfram und Heinrich" confirmed the property of Kloster Herrenalb by charter dated Feb 1270[772].  "Otto nobilis de Eberstein senior" sold revenue from property at Oeweshei
2 CONC m to Speyer cathedral "per manus filiorum nostrorum Ottonis et Wolfelini militum ac Heinrici scolaris" by charter dated 1277[773].  "Otto der ältere Graf von Eberstein" donated property of Kloster Herrenalb, with the consent of "s
2 CONC einer Söhne Otto und Wolfrad", for the souls of "seine verstorbene Gemahlin Beatrix, seinen Vater Eberhard und seine Mutter Cunigunde und seinen Sohn Conrad von Freiburg", by charter dated 1278[774].  A inscription in Herrenalb mo
2 CONC nastery records the death in 1279 of "Dnus Ottho senior…comes de Eberstein"[775].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly KUNIGUNDE von Freiburg, daughter of EGINO [V] Graf von Urach und Freiburg & his wife Adelheid von Neuffen (-before 13 Dec 1244).  "Otto de Eberstein" donated property to Kloster Herrenalb, for the soul of "quondam 
2 CONC uxoris nostre Cunegundis de Friburch", by charter dated 1255[776].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' (Papal dispensation 13 Dec 1244) --- von Teck, daughter of KONRAD [I] Herzog von Teck & his wife ---.  Pope Innocent IV issued a dispensation for the marriage of “Ottonis [...comitis] de Eberstein...episcopi Spiren
2 CONC sis germano” and “nata ducis Debee”, despite “quarta consanguinitatis linea”, dated 13 Dec 1244[777].  It is not known whether this marriage proceeded.  If it died, the bride must have died soon afterwards.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (before 22 Jul 1252) BEATRIX von Krautheim, daughter of WOLFRAD von Krautheim [Bocksberg] & his wife --- (-before 1278).  "Otto de Eberstein cum domina Beatrice nostri…thori socia, domini Wolfradi nobilis de Cruth
2 CONC eim filia" renounced rights in the inheritance of "domini Cunradi…patrui nostri senioris de Cruthein" by charter dated 22 Jul 1252, witnessed by "dominum Kraftonem de Bockesberc, dominum Gotefridum de Hohinloch nobiles…"[778].  "O
2 CONC tto der ältere Graf von Eberstein" donated property of Kloster Herrenalb, with the consent of "seiner Söhne Otto und Wolfrad", for the souls of "seine verstorbene Gemahlin Beatrix, seinen Vater Eberhard und seine Mutter Cunigunde 
2 CONC und seinen Sohn Conrad von Freiburg", by charter dated 1278[779].  
2 CONT
2 CONT   Otto [I] & his first wife had three children: 
2 CONT   Otto [I] & his [first/second] wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
