2 CONT ==The German Revolution==
2 CONT Germany and the Reformation: 1517-1648
2 CONT
2 CONT The decline of the Holy Roman Empire is closely connected with the great 16th-century upheaval in central Europe - that of the Reformation. The German 'princes,' in the many semi-independent territories of the empire, see the reli
2 CONC gious options suddenly on offer as political opportunities.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pope is resented by many as a devious and distant 'intriguer' who drains away money from local church lands and regularly demands more. The Emperor, lord of vast new Habsburg territories, is now also a distant figure with inte
2 CONC rests far beyond the traditional empire. 	  	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Once the turmoil of the 'Reformation' began in the years after 1517, each German prince assessed his 'own' best chance of securing or expanding his territory and his treasury. The resulting conflicts within German-speaking regions
2 CONC  became frequent until the peace of Augsburg in 1555. They then erupt again in the Thirty Years' War of 1618-48.
2 CONT
2 CONT The great dispute soon becomes a European event. But the original flare-up in 1517 is very much a German phenomenon.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.historyworld.net/wrldhis/PlainTextHistories.asp?groupid=2794&HistoryID=ac62&gtrack=pthc#ixzz3PRsHr6zC Read more]
2 CONT
2 CONT During and near the end of the German Revolution (in 1555), a branch of the family, 'The French Branch' settled in Vieux-Ferrette France, with many of the other 'Old Ferrette' or 'Old Sandgau', southern Brabant families, i.e.; Bli
2 CONC nd (Blundt, Blonde, Blount), Libis (Sisbas), Bischoff, etc...
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.worldleadersindex.org/EuropeRegions/GermanFreeCities.html German Free Cities]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.worldstatesmen.org/German_States3.html German States to 1918]
2 CONT
