1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ('''Curator Note: with this profile we have a general problem identifying his wife.  Most site involved with Cuijk identify her as NN or Ida (aka Jutta) Von Cuijk-Arnsberg while other resources identify her as Luka
2 CONC rdis von Nassau''',)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Virneburg (Adelsgeschlecht)'''
2 CONT Die Grafen von Virneburg waren ein Adelsgeschlecht, das im Mittelalter zu den einflussreichen Adelsfamilien im Rheinland gehörte. Sie hatten ihren Stammsitz auf Burg Virneburg. Ihr Territorium war die Grafschaft Virneburg, die im 
2 CONC heutigen linksrheinischen nördlichen Rheinland-Pfalz lag. Mit Heinrich II. von Köln und Heinrich III. von Mainz stellten die Virneburger im 14. Jahrhundert zwei Erzbischöfe und Kurfürsten.
2 CONT > The Counts of Virneburg were a noble family that was among the most influential in the Rhineland during the Middle Ages. Their ancestral seat was Virneburg Castle. Their territory was the County of Virneburg, located in what is 
2 CONC now the northern part of Rhineland-Palatinate on the left bank of the Rhine. In the 14th century, the Virneburgs produced two archbishops and electors: Henry II of Cologne and Henry III of Mainz.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Geschichte'''
2 CONT Der Vater des ersten Grafen von Virneburg, Hermann, war wahrscheinlich Bernard von Virneburg, der von 1042 bis 1061 in Urkunden der Erzbischöfe von Trier als Zeuge aufgeführt wurde.[1]
2 CONT > The father of the first Count of Virneburg, Hermann, was probably Bernard of Virneburg, who was listed as a witness in documents of the Archbishops of Trier from 1042 to 1061.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Das Geschlecht der Grafen von Virneburg erlosch mit Kuno von Virneburg, Sohn von Philipp von Virneburg und Walpurgis von Solms–Lich, Graf zu Virneburg und Neuenahr, Herr zu Saffenberg und Sombreff, im Jahre 1545 und wurde von den 
2 CONC Grafen von Manderscheid beerbt. Doch es war nur ein Teil, den sie erbten, denn der Trierer Fürstbischof hatte den größten Teil (die große und kleine Pellenz, Ort und Burg Monreal, die Höfe Kehrig und Spurzenheim) als erledigtes Le
2 CONC hen eingezogen. Der Rest mit Virneburg selbst fiel an Graf Dietrich IV. von Manderscheid (reg. 1501–1551), den Sohn von Mechthild von Virneburg. Graf Dietrich V. von Manderscheid-Schleiden (reg. 1551–1560) bekam dann den Rest als 
2 CONC Lehen von Trier. Von den Grafen von Manderscheid kam die Grafschaft Virneburg zu Ende des 16. Jahrhunderts durch Heirat an die Grafen von Löwenstein-Wertheim.
2 CONT > The line of the Counts of Virneburg became extinct with Kuno of Virneburg, son of Philipp of Virneburg and Walpurgis of Solms-Lich, Count of Virneburg and Neuenahr, Lord of Saffenberg and Sombreff, in 1545, and was succeeded by 
2 CONC the Counts of Manderscheid. However, they inherited only a portion, as the Prince-Bishop of Trier had confiscated the greater part (the Greater and Lesser Pellenz, the town and castle of Monreal, and the estates of Kehrig and Spur
2 CONC zenheim) as a vacant fief. The remainder, including Virneburg itself, fell to Count Dietrich IV of Manderscheid (reigned 1501–1551), the son of Mechthild of Virneburg. Count Dietrich V of Manderscheid-Schleiden (reigned 1551–1560)
2 CONC  then received the remainder as a fief from Trier. At the end of the 16th century, the County of Virneburg passed from the Counts of Manderscheid to the Counts of Löwenstein-Wertheim through marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wappen'''
2 CONT Stammwappen derer von Virneburg: In Gold sieben (4:3) zu zwei Balken aneinandergereihte rote Rauten (Wecken). Auf dem Helm mit schwarz-roten Decken der verkleinerte Wappenschild zwischen zwei schwarzen Hörnern, die außen mit je dr
2 CONC ei silbernen Kugeln besteckt sind. Die Helmzier variierte anfangs: Rupert III. von Virneburg zeigte 1355 einen schwarzen Flug. Sein Sohn Heinrich führte 1335 den Schild mit blauem Turnierkragen und eine Art Schirmbrett zwischen de
2 CONC m Flug. Seine Brüder Gerhard und Adolf von Virneburg etablierten schließlich als Helmzier den Schild zwischen den Hörnern.[2]
2 CONT > The family coat of arms of the Lords of Virneburg: In gold, seven red lozenges (4:3) arranged in two bars. On the helmet with black and red mantling, the reduced shield between two black horns, each adorned on the outside with t
2 CONC hree silver balls. The crest initially varied: Rupert III of Virneburg displayed a black pair of wings in 1355. His son Heinrich used the shield with a blue tournament collar and a kind of escutcheon between the wings in 1335. His
2 CONC  brothers Gerhard and Adolf of Virneburg finally established the shield between the horns as the crest.[2]
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222006251869&size=medium}} Wappen im Scheiblerschen Wappenbuch
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Stammliste der Familie'''
2 CONT Hermann I. von Virneburg, Graf (1112–????)
2 CONT Hermann II. von Virneburg, Graf (11??–1192) ⚭ NN von Cuyk, Tochter von Gottfried von Cuyk
2 CONT Gottfried von Virneburg, Graf (1192–1204)
2 CONT Hermann III. von Virneburg, Graf (1204–1238) ⚭ (II) Lukardis von Nassau (* um 1175/1180; † vor 1222); ⚭ I: Gebhard IV. von Querfurt, Burggraf von Magdeburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatur (Internal Sourcing)
2 CONT 1. Wilhelm Iwanski: Geschichte der Grafen von Virneburg. Von ihren Anfängen bis auf Robert IV. (1383), Koblenz 1912 (dilibri.de).
2 CONT 2. Karl Klapperich: Die Geschichte des Grafengeschlechtes der Virneburger, Bonn 1920.
2 CONT 3. Peter Brommer: Kleinere Territorien, Herrschaften und Teile auswärtiger Territorien. Nordteil. In: Franz-Josef 4. 4. Heyen (Hrsg.): Geschichte des Landes Rheinland-Pfalz Freiburg im Breisgau, Würzburg 1981, S. 67–70.
2 CONT Friedrich Toepfer: Beilagen XI. Die Grafen von Virneburg. In: ders. (Bearb.): Urkundenbuch für die Geschichte des graeflichen und freiherrlichen Hauses der Voegte von Hunolstein, Bd. I. Jacob Zeiser, Nürnberg 1866, S. 331–334 (Goo
2 CONC gle-Books)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia Contributors. “Virneburg (Adelsgeschlecht).” Wikipedia.org, Wikimedia Foundation, Inc., 4 Dec. 2010, de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virneburg_(Adelsgeschlecht). Accessed 31 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT Hermann II
2 CONT Graf von Virneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Family	NN von Arnsberg 
2 CONT Children 	
2 CONT 1. Gottfried, Graf von Virneburg
2 CONT Internal Sourcing: Schwennicke, Detlev (Ed.), ~Europäische Stammtafeln, J.A. Stargardt Verlag, Marburg.
2 CONT 7:143 (weak)
2 CONT Source: van der Pas, Leo. “Hermann.” Genealogics.org, 2016, www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00107740&tree=LEO. Accessed 31 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT ('''Curator Note: it is noted that in Medlands the dates of birth and death and his wife are different than other sources!''')
2 CONT 1.         GOTTFRIED von Virneburg (-after [1194]).  Graf von Virneburg.  "Godefridus et Fredericus fratres comites de Vernenburgh" donated their castle and county to Johann Archbishop of Trier by charter dated 9 Aug 1187 witnesse
2 CONC d by "Henrici comitis Palatini Reni advocatie ecclesie predicte…Walrami comitis in Spanheim, Conradi de Blankenheim et Wilhelmi domni in Duna…"[3253].  "Laicis: Godefrido comite de Virnburch, Reinbaldo et Gerlaco de Ysenburch, Rud
2 CONC olpho de Malberch" witnessed the charter dated 1192 under which Johann Archbishop of Trier restored property to Stift Carden[3254].  "Gerlacus de Isenburg et eius filiis" agreed that "castri de Altenburg et castri de Coverna infer
2 CONC ioris" were fiefs of the archbishopric of Trier by charter dated to [1194], witnessed by "…Gotfridus comes de Virneburg et frater eius Fridericus…"[3255].  m ---.  The name of Gottfried’s wife is not known.  Gottfried & his wife h
2 CONC ad [two children]: 
2 CONT a)         HERMANN [III] von Virneburg, son of [GOTTFRIED Graf von Virneburg & his wife ---] (-[25 Nov 1238/15 Feb 1242]).  "Comes Hermannus de Virneburch, Fridericus patruus eius, Ernestus et frater eius Henricus…" subscribed the
2 CONC  charter dated 1213 under which "Henricus de Ysenburch" donated property at Cruft to Laach abbey[3269].  This charter indicates that Hermann [III] was the nephew of Graf Friedrich but not that he was the son of Gottfried, although
2 CONC  the fact that he succeeded his uncle as Graf von Virneburg suggests that this is probably the case.  Graf von Virneburg.  Theoderich Archbishop of Trier confirmed the donation of property to Kloster Lonnig made by "domnum Hermann
2 CONC um comitem de Viernburch" by charter dated 1219[3270].  A charter dated 1229 records the agreement between "Hermannum comitem de Virnenburgh" and "Philippum fratrem suum" to divide their territories[3271].  "Hermannus comes de Fir
2 CONC neburg…manu filii nostri Roberti et fratris nostri Philippi et…patrui nostri Friderici" granted "curtem in Cottenheim" to "Sibertus et fratres ipsius" by undated charter[3272].  "Hermannus…comes de Virneburg" donated property "in 
2 CONC Thure et predium in Busse" to Himmerode abbey, with the consent of "filiorum et filiarum mearum", by charter dated 25 Nov 1238, witnessed by "Philippus frater meus, Henricus canonicus Kardonensis et Robertus filii mei…"[3273].  ["
2 CONC Roisemannus nobilis domnus de Kempenich…cum matre nostra Hadewige nobili de Kempenich" confirmed a donation to Kloster Rommersdorf [bei Engers] made by "Fridericus noster consanguineus olim dominus in Virnenburg…cum matertera nost
2 CONC ra conjuge sua domna Lukarde" by charter dated 1263[3274].  It is possible that "Fridericus" in this document is an error for "Hermannus".  The document appears late to refer to Graf Friedrich who is last recorded in 1187 (see abo
2 CONC ve).  The precise relationship between the Kempenich family and Lukardis, wife of Graf Hermann [III], has not been traced.] 
2 CONT m (before 27 Feb 1204) LUKARDIS von Nassau, daughter of RUPRECHT [III] "der Streitbare" Graf von Nassau & his wife Elisabeth von Leiningen Gräfin von Schaumburg (-[31 Aug] before 1222).  "Elysa comitissa dicta de Schowenburg, reli
2 CONC cta…Ruperti comitis de Nassowe" sold property to Kloster Johannisberg, with the consent of "Hermanni comitis de Virneburg et Luccardis conthoralis ipsius filie nostre advocatiam et iudicium ville Steinheim", by charter dated 27 Fe
2 CONC b 1204[3275].  ["Roisemannus nobilis domnus de Kempenich…cum matre nostra Hadewige nobili de Kempenich" confirmed a donation to Kloster Rommersdorf [bei Engers] made by "Fridericus noster consanguineus olim dominus in Virnenburg…c
2 CONC um matertera nostra conjuge sua domna Lukarde" by charter dated 1263[3276].  It is possible that "Fridericus" in this document is an error for "Hermannus".  The document appears late to refer to Graf Friedrich who is last recorded
2 CONC  in 1187 (see above).  The precise relationship between the Kempenich family and Lukardis, wife of Graf Hermann [III], has not been traced.]  [The necrology of Arnstein an der Lahn records the death 31 Aug of "Lucardis comitisse i
2 CONC ncluse"[3277].] 
2 CONT Hermann [III] & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
