1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''OTTO [IV] von Scheyern  (-4/5 Aug 1156, bur Ensdorf). Pfalzgraf von Wittelsbach'''
2 CONT s/o OTTO [III] Graf von Scheyern
2 CONT m HEILIKA von Pettendorf, d/o [Graf] FRIEDRICH von Pettendorf &  [Heilika von Staufen] (-13/14 Sep 1170, bur Ensdorf). ]
2 CONT * 1.         OTTO [V] von Wittelsbach (-Pfullendorf 11 Jul 1183, bur Scheyern) OTTO I Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT * 2.         FRIEDRICH von Wittelsbach (-15 Sep [1198/99], bur Indersdorf). m NN von Wörth, d/o MANGOLD Graf von [Donau-]Wörth 
2 CONT * 3.         ULRICH (-[1160]).  Canon and Archdeacon at Freising Cathedral.  Provost at Innichen.
2 CONT * 4.         KONRAD von Wittelsbach (-Riedfeld [26 Oct] 1200).    Canon at Salzburg Cathedral.  Archbishop of Mainz Cardinal of San Marco. Cardinal-Bishop of Sabina and Bishop of Sora.  Archbishop of Salzburg. Archbishop of Mainz.
2 CONC
2 CONT * 5.         OTTO [VI] von Wittelsbach (-18 Aug 1189, bur Indersdorf).  m BENEDIKTA von Wörth, d/o MANGOLD Graf von [Donau-]Wörth 
2 CONT * 6.         [HEDWIG (-16 Jul 1174). m (<1153)  BERTHOLD [II] Graf von Andechs, s/o BERTHOLD [I] Graf von Andechs &Sophia of Istria (-14 Dec 1188).  Markgraf von Istria 1173.]  
2 CONT * 7.         ADELHEID (-8/9 Feb ----). m OTTO [II] Landgraf von Steffling, s/o OTTO [I] Burggraf von Regensburg & Adelheid von Plötzkau (-16 Aug [1175])
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BAVARIA.htm#OttoIIdied1156
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/135319035/otto_v-von_scheyern
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_IV,_Count_of_Scheyern
2 CONT
2 CONT - http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BAVARIA.htm#OttoIDukedied1183A
2 CONT
2 CONT OTTO [IV] von Scheyern, son of OTTO [III] Graf von Scheyern & his wife --- (-4/5 Aug 1156, bur Ensdorf).  “Perchtoldo de Esgilbach” exchanged property with Freising church, in the presence of “Ottonis advocatis et filii sui Ottoni
2 CONC s”, by undated charter dated to the early 12th century[837].  Wegener conflates Graf Otto [III] and Graf Otto [IV] into one person[838].  Europäische Stammtafeln shows them separately[839].  The latter is more probable from a chro
2 CONC nological point of view.  If it is correct that Graf Otto [III] was the son of Graf Ekkehard, he must have been born just before the death of his father in [1087/88], given the age of his mother.  On the other hand, Otto I Duke of
2 CONC  Bavaria married in [1156/58].  The difference between these two dates would be unusually great if Duke Otto was the son of Otto [III].  No primary sources have so far been found to confirm the matter one way or another.  Otto Bis
2 CONC hop of Freising confirmed an exchange of property, confirmed by “Otto Palatinus comes senior et filius eius Otto”, by undated charter[840].  Vogt of Freising Cathedral.  Vogt von Ensdorf, Ober Pfalz.  He founded Ensdorf Monastery 
2 CONC in 1121, and Indersdorf Convent 1124.  Pfalzgraf von Wittelsbach 1124.  Vogt von Geisenfeld [1130].  The necrology of Thierhaupt records the death "Non Aug" of "Otto com de Wittelspach"[841].  The necrology of Undensdorf records t
2 CONC he death "II Non Aug" of "Otto palatinus pater ducis"[842].  The necrology of Weltenburg records the death "II Non Aug" of "Otto palatinus com"[843]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' HEILIKA von Pettendorf, daughter of [Graf] FRIEDRICH von Pettendorf & his wife [Heilika von Staufen] (-13/14 Sep 1170, bur Ensdorf).  The Fundatio Monasterii Ensdorfensis records the death "1170 XVIII Kal Oct" of "Heylice 
2 CONC palatine", naming her parents "Fridericum et Heylwic" and specifying that they were all buried at Ensdorf[844].  The necrology of Undensdorf records the death "XVIII Kal Oct" of "Hailica palatina"[845]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pfalzgraf Otto [IV] & his wife had [seven] children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT ------
2 CONT ------
2 CONT ------
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto of Wittelsbach, his wife Heilika and Bishop Otto von Bamberg founded the Benedictine monastery in Ensdorf, Bavaria on 5/23/1121.Presently, the monastery is known as Kloster Ensdorf and now houses an environmental station. The
2 CONC  founders tomb of the count Palatine Otto of Wittelsbach and his wife is located on the North wall in the chancel of the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.kloster-ensdorf.de/kloster/geschichte/
2 CONT
2 CONT The St. James the Greater Benedictine monastery was consecrated in 1121 by Count Palatine Otto of Wittelsbach, and Bishop Otto of Bamberg founded. The Count Palatine thereby fulfilled the wish of his father(-in-law), Frederick III
2 CONC . of Pettendorf-Lengenfeld-Hopfenohe († 1119), after a burial place for the family. The Earl, his wife Heilika of Swabia, Heilika his daughter, her husband and her son, Count Palatine Otto Count Palatine Frederick von Wittelsbach,
2 CONC  who was at the age occurred as a lay brother in the monastery Indersdorf were eventually buried in the monastery. Also buried here, the sister Heilikas, Heilwig Lengenfeld and her husband Gebhard von Leuchtenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kloster_Ensdorf
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto IV, Count of Wittelsbach (c. 1083-4 August 1156) was a descendant of Count Otto II of Scheyern and Richgard of Weimar-Istrien. Otto entitled himself after Wittelsbach Castle near Aichach from 1116 onwards, served Henry V, Hol
2 CONC y Roman Emperor and was Count palatine of Bavaria since 1120.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto's sons with Heilika of Lengenfeld-Pettendorf were Otto I, the first Wittelsbach duke of Bavaria and Conrad of Wittelsbach, archbishop of Mainz and Salzburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
