2 ADDR
1 OCCU Cardinal, Ärkebiskop i Mainz 1161-77, + 1183-1200 och i Sal<burg 1177-83, Erzbischof von Mainz (Konrad I.) und Salzburg (Konrad III.) und Kardinalbischof.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Conrad of Wittelsbach (born ca. 1120/1125 — died 25 October 1200) was the Archbishop of Mainz (as Conrad I) and Archchancellor of Germany from 20 June 1161 to 1165 and again from 1183 to his death. He was also a ca
2 CONC rdinal of the Roman Catholic Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Otto IV, Count of Wittelsbach, and brother of Otto I of Bavaria, he studied in Salzburg and Paris. At the Council of Lodi in 1161, Frederick Barbarossa appointed him archbishop of Mainz to end a schism between Rudolf of
2 CONC  Zähringen and Christian von Buch in that see. At that same council, Barbarossa appointed Victor IV antipope in opposition to Pope Alexander III. After Victor's death in 1164, Rainald of Dassel, the archbishop of Cologne, chose as
2 CONC  antipope Paschal III at Lucca. Conrad refused to support the new antipope and consequently fell out with Barbarossa. He fled to France and then Rome in 1165 and his see was bestowed on Christian von Buch, though Alexander III sti
2 CONC ll recognised him as legal archbishop. On 18 December, the pope made him cardinal priest of San Marcello al Corso and then cardinal bishop of Sabina. The pope later created him bishop of Sora in Campania. Conrad fled before Christ
2 CONC ian took Rome with an imperial army.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the Treaty of Venice of 1177, the pope was constrained to recognised Christian as the legitimate archbishop of Mainz, but Conrad was compensated with the archdiocese of Salzburg (as Conrad III). Conrad never, however, ceased to
2 CONC  regard himself as anything but the rightful archbishop of Mainz. When Christian died in 1183, Conrad could again assume his archiepiscopal responsibilities in that city, which, in 1160, had been deprived by the emperor of its cha
2 CONC rter for the murder of the archbishop Arnold of Selenhofen. The fortifications had then been levelled, but Conrad rebuilt them and renovated Mainz Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April or May 1187, at the Diet of Gelnhausen, Conrad convinced his fellow bishops to support the emperor's cause against Rome. In the Fall of 1188, the council was held at Mainz which announced the Third Crusade. Conrad led an 
2 CONC army on Crusade in 1197, the same year the Emperor Henry VI died. Conrad, with the other imperial princes, had elected his infant son Frederick king in 1196. While Conrad was in the Holy Land acting as legate for Pope Celestine II
2 CONC I, he intervened in the princely succession of Antioch. He tried to get Raymond-Roupen recognised as the successor of Bohemond III instead of Bohemond IV. On 6 January 1199, with papal permission, Conrad crowned Leo II, Lord of th
2 CONC e Mountains, King of Armenia as a vassal of the Holy Roman Empire. Later that year, he returned equipped with new legatine power by Pope Innocent III. He succeeded in establishing an armistice in April 1200 between the competing f
2 CONC actions in Germany, namely the Hohenstaufen and the Welf.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Cardinal-Bishop of Sabina, he signed the papal bulls issued between March 18, 1166 and November 6, 1199. After the election to the papacy of Cardinal Ubaldo Allucingoli (Pope Lucius III) in 1181 he became new dean of the Sacred
2 CONC  College of Cardinals.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was returning to Outremer when he died in Riedfeld near Neustadt on the Aisch, in what was then Hungary. He was buried in the cathedral he had expanded.
