2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT ==Irmgard von Baden 
2 CONT '''F, #41805, b. 1270, d. 8 February 1320'''
2 CONT Last Edited=19 Feb 2017
2 CONT '''Irmgard von Baden was born in 1270.1 She was the daughter''' of Rudolf I Markgraf von Baden and Kunigunde von Eberstein. '''She married Eberhard I Graf von Württemberg''', son of Ulrich I Graf von Württemberg and Matilde von Ba
2 CONC den, '''on 21 June 1296.1 She died on 8 February 1320.'''1
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1. [S130] Wikipedia, online http;//www.wikipedia.org. Hereinafter cited as Wikipedia.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p4181.htm#i41805
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT '''Eberhard I''' (13 March 1265, in Stuttgart – 5 June 1325, in Stuttgart) was Count of Württemberg from 1279 until his death. '''He was nicknamed 'der Erlauchte' or the Illustrious Highness.'''
2 CONT Life
2 CONT '''Eberhard's''' half-brother and predecessor Ulrich II took office at the age of about eleven years. It is generally assumed that he stood under the guardianship and regency of Count Hartmann I of Grüningen. Ulrich II died in 127
2 CONC 9 and his guardian Hartmann in 1280, allowing '''Eberhard''' to exercise unrestricted reign of the County of Württemberg from 1280.
2 CONT His father, Ulrich I, had extended the Württemberg territory and the anti-king Henry Raspe had legitimized his conquests. However, when Rudolph I was elected king, these territories had to be returned to the Empire. Rudolph create
2 CONC d bailiwicks to administer the reclaimed imperial territories. He appointed his brother-in-law Albert II of Hohenberg-Rotenburg as vogt of the new bailiwick of Lower Swabia. Rudolph's aim was to re-establish the Duchy of Swabia, w
2 CONC hich had been leaderless after the execution in 1268 of Conradin, the last member of the Hohenstaufen dynasty. To this end, he appointed his young son Rudolph II as duke of Swabia. '''Eberhard''' resisted these measures. Despite b
2 CONC eing weaker, he managed exploit the situation after Rudolph's death in 1291 and achieve military successes against Albert II of Hohenberg-Rotenburg.
2 CONT Rudolph's successor Adolph of Nassau, did not try to extend his power base into Swabia. After Adolph was overthrown in 1298, '''Eberhard''' promised to support his successor Albert I. In return, Albert appointed him vogt of Lower 
2 CONC Swabia. '''Eberhard''' used this position to safeguard his territorial claims. It was not until 1305 that he again came into military conflict with Albert I.
2 CONT '''Eberhard''' supported the Bohemian nobility in their struggle against Albert I and his successor Henry VII. Vogt Konrad IV von Weinberg, who acted on the orders of Henry VII, declared war on '''Eberhard''', causing much distres
2 CONC s in Württemberg. Württemberg was saved from defeat by the death of Henry VII on 24 August 1313 and the 1314 elections Louis IV and Frederick the Fair as rival kings. By maneuvering tactically between the two, '''Eberhard''' manag
2 CONC ed to offset his territorial losses and even gain some more territories. His participation in the war in Bohemia brought in additional funding, which he used to purchase land and towns in Swabia from impoverished noble families, f
2 CONC or example, the count palatine of Tübingen.
2 CONT '''Eberhard I''' made Stuttgart the capital of Württemberg. He died in 1325 and was buried in the Stiftskirche in Stuttgart.
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''Eberhard''' was the son of Count Ulrich I of Württemberg, who died a few weeks before his birth. His mother was Agnes of Silesia-Legnica, a daughter of Boleslaw II Rogatka.[1] She probably died at his birth; some sources speak 
2 CONC of a caesarean section.
2 CONT '''Eberhard was married three times.'''
2 CONT First marriage
2 CONT The identity of the first wife is uncertain. Martin Crucius thought she may have been Adelaide of Werdenberg-Heiligenberg from Sigmaringen. Other authors think that she was a member of the noble Hohenberg family, but they may be c
2 CONC onfused with Matilda of Hohenberg, who married his son and successor Ulrich III. A third theory holds that she was a daughter of the Dukes of Teck. The fact that his son Ulrich III acquired Sigmaringen in 1325 and that his daughte
2 CONC r Agnes married Count Henry of Werdenberg-Sargans-Trochtelfingen both point to a close relationship between '''Eberhard''' and the Werdenberg dynasty.
2 CONT This marriage produced a son and a daughter:
2 CONT *Ulrich (born: after 1285; died: 1315)
2 CONT *Agnes (born: before 1300; died: before 1349), married Henry of Werdenberg-Sargans-Trochtelfingen
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT '''Eberhard's''' second wife was Margaret, a daughter of Duke Frederick III of Lorraine. From this marriage, '''Eberhard''' had a son:
2 CONT *Ulrich III (born: between 1286 and 1291; died: 11 July 1344), Count of Württemberg from 1325 to 1344
2 CONT '''Third marriage'''
2 CONT '''Eberhard's third wife was Irmengard, a daughter''' of Margrave Rudolph I of Baden-Baden. '''From this marriage, Eberhard had three daughters''':
2 CONT *Agnes (born: around 1295; died: 1317), countess by marriage of Oettingen
2 CONT *Adelaide Matilda (born: between 1295 and 1300; died: 3 September 1342), married Kraft II of Hohenlohe (d. 3 May 1344)
2 CONT *Irmengard (born: after 1300; died: 1329), countess by marriage of Hohenberg
2 CONT Illegitimate
2 CONT The clergyman Ulrich von Höfingen is believed to have been an illegitimate son of '''Eberhard's.'''
2 CONT References
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eberhard_I,_Count_of_W%C3%BCrttemberg
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT C.      HERREN von HOHENLOHE in WEIKERSHEIM
2 CONT *4.         KRAFT [II] von Hohenlohe (-8 May 1344).  "Dominus noster Crafto de Hohenloch, Cunradus, Crafto filii eiusdem domini nostri…" witnessed the charter dated 13 Jan 1290 under which Konrad Lesch von Uffigheim sold property 
2 CONC to the Teutonic Knights at Mergentheim[1204].  The Notæ Fuerstenfeldenses record that Ludwig Duke of Bavaria was killed "apud civitatem Nurenberg in hastiludio per Kraftonen de Hohenloch" and buried at Fürstenfeld next to his moth
2 CONC er[1205], dated to 23 Nov 1290 by other sources.  "Kraft der edel man von Hohenloch…Margereten von Truhendingen unser…wirtinne und Cuunraden unsers sunes, den wir haben bi unser ersten wirtinne seliger von Wertheim, und auch Kraft
2 CONC es unsers sunes" sold property at Stalldorf to the Teutonic Order at Mergentheim by charter dated 1292[1206].  "Krafto nobilis de Hohenloch, Agnes matrona nobilis nostra collateralis et filii nostri Cunradus et Krafto" sold proper
2 CONC ty to Kloster Schönthal by charter dated 3 Jul 1294[1207].  "Krafto nobilis de Hohenloch…cum Cunrado, Kraftone et Gotfrido filiis nostris" donated rights over the churches of Belsenburg and Ingelfingen to Stift Öhringen by charter
2 CONC  dated 6 Jul 1307[1208].  "Kraft von Hohenloch, Adelheid sin eliche Husfrowe" exchanged his part of Ritzingen with "Gotfrid von Hohenloch unser…bruder und Elizabet sin eliche Husfrowe" for Königshofen by charter dated 2 Oct 1321, 
2 CONC which names "Boppe von Everstein unser Oheim" as guarantor[1209].  "Craft von Hohenloch, frowe Adelheid sin elich Husfrowe, Tochter Graven Eberhards von Wirtenberg" donated property to Kloster Gnadenthal by charter dated 7 Jan 132
2 CONC 8[1210].  m (before 21 Dec 1306) ADELHEID von Württemberg, '''daughter of EBERHARD I "der Erlauchte" Graf von Württemberg & his wife Irmgard von Baden''' (1295-13 Sep 1342, bur Gnadenthal).  "Kraft von Hohenloch, Adelheid sin elic
2 CONC he Husfrowe" exchanged his part of Ritzingen with "Gotfrid von Hohenloch unser…bruder und Elizabet sin eliche Husfrowe" for Königshofen by charter dated 2 Oct 1321, which names "Boppe von Everstein unser Oheim" as guarantor[1211].
2 CONC   "Craft von Hohenloch, frowe Adelheid sin elich Husfrowe, Tochter Graven Eberhards von Wirtenberg" donated property to Kloster Gnadenthal by charter dated 7 Jan 1328[1212].  Kraft [II] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT **a)         KRAFT [III] von Hohenlohe (-16 Nov 1371).  m (before 12 Mar 1340) ANNA von Leuchtenberg, daughter of ULRICH [I] Landgraf von Leuchtenberg & his second wife Anna von Nürnberg (-11 Jun 1390).  Kraft [III] & his wife had
2 CONC  children: 
2 CONT ***i)          KRAFT [IV] von Hohenlohe (-24 Nov 1399).  m firstly (before 28 Oct 1370) AGNES von Ziegenhain, daughter of GOTTFRIED Graf von Ziegenhain & his wife ---.  m secondly (before 23 Mar 1374) ELISABETH von Sponheim, daugh
2 CONC ter of HEINRICH [II] Graf von Sponheim & his wife Adelheid von Katzenelnbogen.  Kraft [IV] & his first wife had children: 
2 CONT ****(a)       ANNA von Hohenlohe (-Kirchheim (11 Oct 1410, bur Kirchheim).  Heiress of Kirchheim[-Bolanden] and Stauf.  m (1385) as his first wife, PHILIPP I Graf von Nassau-Weilburg, son of JOHANN I Graf von Nassau-Weilburg & his
2 CONC  second wife Johanna von Saarbrücken (1368-Wiesbaden 2 Jul 1429, bur Kloster Klarenthal). 
2 CONT ***ii)         ALBRECHT von Hohenlohe (-1429). 
2 CONT ***-        see below. 
2 CONT ***iii)        ADELHEID von Hohenlohe (-before 6 Nov 1370).  “Graf Hainrich von Fúrstemberg lantgraf in Bare...und min vetter selgi graf Cunrat von Fúrstemberg” founded a mass at Neidingen, for “Adelhait seligen grafennen von Fúrs
2 CONC temberg geborn von Hohenloh...des vorgnanten graf Hainrich...frowen”, by charter dated 6 Nov 1370[1213].  m as his first wife, HEINRICH [IV] Graf von Fürstenberg, son of HEINRICH [III] Graf von Fürstenberg & his wife Anna von Mont
2 CONC fort (-[15 Aug] 1408). 
2 CONT ***iv)        ANNA von Hohenlohe (-1 Jun 1434).  Gerhard Bishop of Würzburg confirmed the grant of “der Burg und Stadt Weickersheim” made by “seinem Schwiegervater Ulrich von Hohenlohe” to “Konrad von Weinsberg” by charter dated 1
2 CONC 391[1214].  “Anna von Weinsperg geborne von Hohenloch...für sich und ihre Tochter Margret” mortgaged “die Kirchen und Priester zu Kregling...” by charter dated 15 Sep 1398, sealed by “Conrat Herr zu Weinsperg, der obengenannten An
2 CONC na Eheherr”[1215].  “Konrad Herr zu Winsperg und Anna geborne von Hohenloh seine Ehefrau, Graf und Herr Heinrich von Schwarzburg für sich und seine Ehefrau Margaretha geborne von Bruneck Tochter der Anna von Weinsberg” donated har
2 CONC vest to “Unserer lieben Frauen in Bieberern” by charter dated 29 Jun 1401[1216].  m firstly KONRAD von Brauneck, son of --- (-[1390]).  m secondly as his first wife, KONRAD Herr von Weinsberg, son of ENGELHARD Herr von Weinsberg &
2 CONC  his wife Anna von Leiningen (-18 Jan 1448, bur Kloster Schönthal). 
2 CONT **b)         IRMGARD von Hohenlohe (-11 May 1372).  m firstly KONRAD III Burggraf von Nürnberg, son of FRIEDRICH IV Burggraf von Nürnberg & his wife Margareta von Görz (-3 Apr 1334).  m secondly (before 4 Jan 1337) as his second w
2 CONC ife, GERLACH Graf von Nassau-Wiesbaden, son of ADOLF Graf von Nassau King of Germany & his wife Imagina von Isenburg-Limburg (-1 Jan 1371, bur Kloster Liebenau).  
2 CONT From: https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANCONIA.htm#HeinrichHohenlohediedafter1207A
2 CONT
