1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry IV, Duke of Limburg From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry IV (1195 – 25 February 1247) was the duke of Limburg and count of Berg from 1226 to his death. He was the son of Waleran III, count of Luxembourg and duke of Limburg, and Cunigunda, daughter of Frederick I, Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally lord of Montjoie, he married Irmgard of Berg, heiress of the County of Berg, a daughter of the count Adolf VI, who died at the Siege of Damietta in 1218. Irmgard and Henry could not immediately inherit the county, as it
2 CONC  was held by Engelbert I, Archbishop of Cologne. Engelbert being the principal adviser of Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor, he was confirmed in the county for life, paying a rent to Henry and Ermengard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbert was assassinated on 7 November 1225 and Henry inherited Berg, inheriting Limburg a little while later. He then entrusted Montjoie to his brother Waleran, who already held Faulquemont. He then fought count Frederick of Is
2 CONC enberg, his brother-in-law, who was suspected of the assassination of Engelbert. In 1228, took part in the Sixth Crusade to the Holy Land. Upon returning to Germany, he made war on the archbishop of Cologne, Konrad von Hochstaden 
2 CONC between 1238 and 1241. He was counted among the partisans of the Hohenstaufen.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Imgard of Berg had 2 sons :
2 CONT * Waleran, his successor in Limburg
2 CONT * Adolf, his successor in Berg.
2 CONT
2 CONT References "Lower Rhine Nobility". "Lotharingian Nobility".
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_V_of_Luxembourg Wikipedia_EN] [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/LIMBURG.htm#HeinrichIVdied1247 Medlands] http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00026502&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich IV. von Limburg (* ca. 1200; † 25. Februar 1246) war seit 1221 Herr von Monschau, regierte von 1225 bis 1246 die Grafschaft Berg, war ab 1226 Herzog von Limburg-Nieder-Lothringen und Vogt von Deutz. Leben [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT Er war der älteste Sohn des Herzogs Walram IV. von Limburg und Niederlothringen und Kunigunde von Monschau. Seine Brüder waren Walram Herr von Monschau und Poilvache, Heinrich V. Graf von Luxemburg und Gerhard Graf von Durbuy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seine Kinder waren Walram V. († 14. Oktober 1279) und Adolf IV. Graf von Berg († 1259).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_IV,_Duke_of_Limburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry IV (1195 – 25 February 1247) was the duke of Limburg and count of Berg from 1226 to his death. He was the son of Waleran III, count of Luxembourg and duke of Limburg, and Cunigunda, daughter of Frederick I, Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Imgard of Berg had 2 sons : Waleran, his successor in Limburg Adolf, his successor in Berg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Henry V, Count of Luxembourg From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry V the Blond (1216 – 24 December 1281), called the Great, was the count of Luxembourg, Laroche and Arlon from 1247 to his death and the count of Namur between 1256 and 1264 as Henry III. He was the son and successor of Walera
2 CONC n III of Limburg and Ermesinda of Luxembourg. In 1240 Henry married Marguerite, daughter of Henry II of Bar and Philippa of Dreux who was the great-granddaughter of King Louis VI of France. Henry's marriage to Margaret brought him
2 CONC  Ligny-en-Barrois as her dowry, though, by a clause in the marriage contract, it remained under the feudal suzerainty of the county of Bar. In contempt of this, Henry paid homage in 1256 to Theobald II of Navarre, in his capacity 
2 CONC as Count of Champagne. Henry's brother-in-law, Theobald II of Bar, took advantage of the conflict then raging between Frederick III of Lorraine and the bishops of Metz. Henry V was a partisan of the duke and so Theobald took the s
2 CONC ide of the bishop. Henry was captured in battle at Prény on 14 September 1266. On 8 September 1268, King Louis IX arbitrated between the two counts and Henry was freed and repossessed of Ligny, but under the suzerainty of the Barr
2 CONC ois. In 1256, Henry seized Namur while the reigning margrave, Baldwin II, was also reigning emperor in Constantinople. Baldwin relinquished his rights to Namur to Guy of Dampierre, Count of Flanders, who retook the margraviate fro
2 CONC m Henry. The two parties made peace and Guy married Henry's daughter. From Margaret he had the following issue: Henry VI, (d. 1288) Count of Luxembourg Waleran I (d. 1288), Count of Ligny & Roussy Isabelle (1247-1298), married Guy
2 CONC  of Dampierre Philippa (1252-1311), married John II, Count of Holland Margaret Felicitas Joanna (d. 1310), Abbess of Clairefontaine He also had at least three bastard sons, including: Henry, bastard of Luxembourg (d. 1288), marrie
2 CONC d Isabelle of Houffalize, heiress of Houffalize
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Henry V the Blond (1216 – 24 December 1281), called the Great, was the count of Luxembourg, Laroche and Arlon from 1247 to his death and the count of Namur between 1256 and 1264 as Henry III. He was the son and successor of Walera
2 CONC n III of Limburg and Ermesinda of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1240 Margaret, daughter of Henry II, Count of Bar, who brought him Ligny-en-Barrois as her dowry, though, by a clause in the marriage contract, it remained under the feudal suzerainty of the county of Bar. In contemp
2 CONC t of this, Henry paid homage in 1256 to Theobald II of Navarre, in his capacity as Count of Champagne. Henry's brother-in-law, Theobald II of Bar, took advantage of the conflict then raging between Frederick III of Lorraine and th
2 CONC e bishops of Metz. Henry V was a partisan of the duke and so Theobald took the side of the bishop. Henry was captured in battle at Prény on 14 September 1266. On 8 September 1268, King Louis IX arbitrated between the two counts an
2 CONC d Henry was freed and repossessed of Ligny, but under the suzerainty of the Barrois.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1256, Henry seized Namur while the reigning margrave, Baldwin II, was also reigning emperor in Constantinople. Baldwin relinquished his rights to Namur to Guy of Dampierre, Count of Flanders, who retook the margraviate from Hen
2 CONC ry. The two parties made peace and Guy married Henry's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Margaret he had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry VI, (d. 1288) Count of Luxembourg  Waleran I (d. 1288), Count of Ligny & Roussy  Isabelle (1247-1298), married Guy de Dampierre  Philippa of Luxembourg (1252-1311), married John II, Count of Holland  Margaret  Felicitas  Joa
2 CONC nna (d. 1310), Abbess of Clairefontaine  He also had at least three bastard sons, including:
