2 CONC erich filius eius"[296].  A charter dated 1104 records donations to Oberaltaich by "Adelheit advocata et tres filii eius Udalrich clericus, Fridreich advocatus matricula Tyberine civitatis Hartwich" and "Leukart de Windberg et duo
2 CONC  fili eius Bertoldus, Albertus"[297].  The primary source which confirms her second marriage has not yet been identified.  "Adelhaid commitissa…cum filio suo Altmanno" donated property to Suben priory by charter dated to before 11
2 CONC 24[298].  '''married firstly FRIEDRICH Domvogt von Regensburg,''' son of FRIEDRICH [I] & his wife [Tuta] von Regensburg ([1055/70]-[1096]).  '''married secondly as his second wife, UDALSCHALK Graf im Lurngau und Gündlkofen [Hirsch
2 CONC berg],''' son of HARTWIG [II] Graf an der unteren Ampar & his wife Avisa --- (-20 Nov [1115]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poppo_II,_Margrave_of_Carniola Poppo II, Margrave of Carniola, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Poppo was the son of Margrave Ulric I of Carniola, whom he succeeded upon his death in 1070. His mother Sophia was a daughter of King Béla I of Hungary. He was thus of royal blood.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Richgard (lt: Richardis), daughter of Count Engelbert of Sponheim (de), who governed Istria until his death on 1 April 1096. According to the 1170 Historia Welforum chronicle, Poppo and Richgard had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia of Istria (d. 1132), married Count Berthold II of Andechs, mother of Margrave Berthold I of Istria (1110/1122–1188)
2 CONT Hedwig, married firstly Count Herman I of Winzenburg and secondly Count Adalbert II of Bogen. Poppo remained a loyal supporter of the Salian emperor Henry IV during the Investiture Controversy. Because of his lack of surviving son
2 CONC s, he was succeeded by his younger brother Ulric II.
2 CONT
