2 TITL Henryk II "Pobozny" Slaski
1 NOTE Henry II the Pious (Polish: Henryk II Pobozny, German: Heinrich II der Fromme; ca. 1196 - 9 April 1241), was a Piast Duke of Silesia-Breslau, Krakow and Southern Greater Poland from 1238 until his death. During 1238-1239 he served
2 CONC  as a regent of two Piast Duchies: Sandomierz and Opole-Raciborz.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of Henry I the Bearded, Duke of Breslau, by his wife (and later Saint) Hedwig, daughter of Berthold IV, Duke of Merania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heir of Breslau. Co-Ruler of his father
2 CONT
2 CONT Monarchy of the Silesian HenriesHenry was the second son of the Ducal couple, but soon he became in the eldest child of the family when the first-born Boleslaw died in 1206. Seven years later (1213) the tragic death of his younger
2 CONC  surviving brother Konrad during a hunt let Henry as the sole heir of Lower Silesia. Around 1218 his father arranged his marriage with Princess Anna, daughter of King Ottokar I of Bohemia; this union allowed Henry to interfere act
2 CONC ively in the international politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry the Bearded quickly began his efforts to let his sole surviving son as the universal heir of his patrimony, and since 1222, the young prince appears signed documents along with his father. Two years later, he had already a s
2 CONC eparate stamp and his own notary. In 1227 during a meeting of Piast Dukes in Gasawa, Henry the Bearded and the High Duke Leszek the White were trapped in an ambush, as a result of which Leszek was killed and the Silesian Duke was 
2 CONC seriously wounded; then was the first time that the government resting on the shoulders of young Henry. The second time happened two years later, when Henry the Bearded was capture by Konrad I of Masovia. During these regencies He
2 CONC nry II's performance was perfect, thanks to the early years when his father entrusted him with this type of responsibility. During 1229-30, the Regent led a military expedition in order to recover and secure the possession of Lubu
2 CONC sz, and in 1233-34 Henry actively support his father's affairs in Prussia and Greater Poland. The increasing and close cooperation with his father derived in the official nomination of Henry as co-rule of his father in 1234. Since
2 CONC  them, were a formal separation of powers: Henry the Bearded styled as Duke of Krakow and Silesia, and Henry as Duke of Silesia and Greater Poland. However, his reign alone had to wait until the death of Henry the Bearded on 19 Ma
2 CONC rch 1238.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign Alone
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of Silesian PiastsAlthough at the time of his father's death Henry was about forty-years-old, he took possession over his inheritance with some complications. Actually, the first problem was the issue of his successio
2 CONC n. The strong authority of Henry the Bearded could secure the hereditary rule in his bloodline only over Lower Silesia. In the Southern Greater Poland and Krakow was ruled by the election between the Piast princes (although exist 
2 CONC a testament of former Duke of Greater Poland and Krakow, Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks, who left all his lands to Henry the Bearded, but this was ignored by Konrad of Masovia and Wladyslaw Odonic). In the case of Opole-Raciborz and 
2 CONC Sandomierz, Henry could retain his authority as a regent during the minority of their rulers Mieszko II and Boleslaw V; however one year later (1239) Henry was compelled to resign to the regency, although he remained on good terms
2 CONC  with the Dukes of Opole and Sandomierz, but also managed to retain Kalisz and Wielun. However, the in the north was more complicated. The Margrave Otto III of Brandenburg, using the death of Henry the Bearded as a pretext, took a
2 CONC n important fortress in Santok and sieged Lubusz. Among this, he also inherited from his father the disputes with Konrad of Masovia and Wladyslaw Odonic and with the Church, leaded Pelka, Archbishop of Gniezno, who claimed the ben
2 CONC efits promised by Henry the Bearded.
2 CONT
2 CONT But fortunately for Henry, the situation changed unexpectedly after the death of Wladyslaw Odonic on 5 June 1239, leaving two minor sons, Przemysl I and Boleslaw. Using these circumstances, the Silesian Duke took the majority of O
2 CONC donic's possessions (including Gniezno), leaving to Odonic's sons Naklo nad Notecia and Ujscie. Henry's next steps however, were dangerous: he abandoned the traditional alliance of his family with the House of Hohenstaufen and sup
2 CONC ported Pope Gregory IX, which immediately finished his disputes with the Church[1]. Then, to ended his conflicts with Konrad of Masovia, Henry arranged the marriages of two of his daughters with two Konrad's sons: the eldest, Gert
2 CONC rude, with Boleslaw, and the second, Constance, with Casimir. In 1239, Henry lost the fortress occupied in Santok by Brandenburg after his defeat in the Battle of Lubusz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mongol invasion. Battle of Legnica and Death
2 CONT It seemed that the most difficult moments for Henry are done. But the worst was yet to come. At the East, a new dangerous opponent appeared: the Mongols, under the leadership of Batu Khan, who, after the defeat and destruction of 
2 CONC Russian Grand Principality of Kiev chose as the next target Hungary. Batu Khan rightly recognized that in the event of war with Hungary firstly he had to take control over Poland. So he send there an army of 10 000 men under the l
2 CONC eadership of Orda. Already in January 1241 Batu send to Lublin and Zawichost some reconnaissance troops, but the real invasion was launched a month later. In Lesser Poland the Mongols didn't have an equal opponent, defeating and k
2 CONC illed almost all the Krakow and Sandomierz nobility in the Battle of Tursko (13 February) and the Battles of Tarczek and Chmielnik(18 March) where, among the casualties, were the voivode of Kraków, Wlodzimierz and the castellan C
2 CONC lement of Brzeznica. After this all Lesser Poland, including Krakow and Sandomierz, were in the hands of the Mongols.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry didn't wait for the promised aid from Western rulers and began to concentrate the survived Lesser Poland troops and his own Silesian and Greater Poland troops in Legnica. Europe's rulers, more interested in the struggles bet
2 CONC ween Empire and Papacy, ignored Henry's requests for help. The only foreign troops who joined him were of King Wenceslaus I of Bohemia and the combined forced of both Knights Templars and Hospitallers, but at the last moment they 
2 CONC stopped their troops close to Legnica, probably fearing that the Christian Army would become an easy prey to the Mongolian troops. The battle took place on 9 April 1241; Henry was defeated and killed in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT The defeat was widely blamed on the Europeans monarchs, who refused to help, and the unexpected and humiliating escape from the battle of Mieszko II the Fat. There are two descriptions of Henry's death, one submitted by Jan Dlugos
2 CONC z (today considered dubious), and the second by C. de Brigia in his Historii Tartatorum, who, based on relates of direct witnesses, is now considered more reliable. Fortunately for Poland, the Mongols didn't intend to occupy the c
2 CONC ountry, since shortly afterward they went through Moravia to Hungary, wanting to connect with the main army of Batu Khan. Henry's naked and decapitated body could only be identified by his wife, thanks to a unique anatomical defec
2 CONC t: on his left foot, he had six toes, which was confirmed at the opening of his tomb in 1832. Henry was buried in his Franciscan Church of St. Jakub in Wroclaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite only three years of rule, Henry remained in the memories of Greater Poland and Krakow as the perfect Christian Knight and Lord, whose brilliant career was stopped by his early death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Issue
2 CONT By 1218, Henry married with Anna (ca. 1204 - 23 June 1265), daughter of King Ottokar I of Bohemia. They had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude (ca. 1218/20 - 23/30 April 1247), married by 1232 with Boleslaw I of Masovia. 
2 CONT Constance (ca. 1221 - ca. 21 February 1257), married by 1239 to Casimir I of Kuyavia. 
2 CONT Boleslaw II the Bald (ca. 1220/25 - 25/31 December 1278). 
2 CONT Mieszko (ca. 1223/27 - 1242). 
2 CONT Henry III the White (1227/30 - 3 December 1266). 
2 CONT Konrad (1228/31 - 6 August ca. 1274). 
2 CONT Elizabeth (ca. 1232 - 16 January 1265), married in 1244 to Przemysl I of Greater Poland. 
2 CONT Agnes (ca. 1236 - 14 May aft. 1278), Abbess of St.Clara in Trebnitz (aft. 1277). 
2 CONT Wladyslaw (1237 - 27 April 1270), Chancellor of Bohemia (1256), Bishop of Passau (1265) and Archbishop of Salzburg (1265-70). 
2 CONT Hedwig (ca. 1238/41 - 3 April 1318), Abbess of St.Clara in Trebnitz. 
2 CONT
