2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla was the eldest son of King Andrew II of Hungary and his first wife, Gertrude of Merania. Upon Pope Innocent III request, the ecclesiastic and temporal dignitaries of the Kingdom of Hungary took an oath before his birth that t
2 CONC hey would accept him as his father's successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The infant Béla was probably present when a group of conspirators murdered his mother on 28 September 1213. Following the murder, his father ordered only the execution of the conspirators' leader and forgave the other members of t
2 CONC he group, which resulted in Béla's emerging antipathy against his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning of 1214, Béla was engaged to a daughter of Tzar Boril of Bulgaria. Shortly afterwards, he was crowned junior king. When his father left for a Crusade in August 1217, his maternal uncle, Archbishop Berthold of Kalo
2 CONC csa took Béla to the fortress of Steyr in Styria and he returned to Hungary one year later, following his father's return from the Holy Land.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Rex iunior
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1220, Béla married Maria Laskarina, a daughter of the Emperor Theodore I Laskaris of Nicaea and his father entrusted him with the government of Slavonia. However, King Andrew II, who had arranged Béla's marriage during his retu
2 CONC rn from the Crusade, persuaded Béla to separate from his wife in 1222. Pope Honorius III, however, denied to declare their marriage null and void; therefore Béla took back his wife and escaped to Austria fearing of his father's an
2 CONC ger. Finally, King Andrew II made an agreement with his son with the mediation of the Pope and Béla took over again the government of Slavonia, Dalmatia and Croatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT As governor, Béla commenced, with the authorization of the Pope, to take back the royal domains that King Andrew II had granted to his partisans during the first half of his reign. He laid siege to Klis, the fortress of a turbulen
2 CONC t Croatian baron who had to surrender.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1226, his father entrusted him with the government of Transylvania where he assisted the missionary work of the Blackfriars among the Cuman tribes who settled down in the territories west of the Dniester River. As a result of t
2 CONC heir missionary work, two chieftains of the Cumans, Bartz and Membrok were baptized and they acknowledged Béla's overlordship around 1228. In the meantime, Béla began to organise the Banat of Szörény, a march of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1228, he commenced to revise his father's "needless and fruitless" donations in the whole territory of the kingdom with the authorisation of his father. However, his military failure in Halych, when assisting his younger brothe
2 CONC r, Andrew, weakened his influence and King Andrew II put an end to the revision of his former donations. During the early 1230s, Béla took part in the military expeditions of his father against Halych and Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT His relation with his father even worsened, when King Andrew II married, on 14 May 1234, Beatrice D'Este, who was thirty years his younger.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The first years of his reign
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father died on 21 September 1235, Béla ascended the throne without any opposition and Archbishop Robert of Esztergom crowned him on 14 October in Székesfehérvár. Shortly afterwards, he accused his young stepmother and his
2 CONC  father's main advisor, Denis, Apud's son of adultery and ordered their arrest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla's main purpose was to restore the royal power that had weakened during his father's rule; e.g., he ordered the burning of his advisors' seats, because he wanted to force them to stand in the presence of the king. As he also w
2 CONC anted to strengthen the position of the towns, he confirmed the charter of Székesfehérvár and granted new privileges to several key towns in the kingdom (Pest, Nagyszombat, Selmecbánya, Korpona, Zólyom, Bars, and Esztergom).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He sent Friar Julian to find the Magyar tribes who had remained in their eastern homeland. Friar Julian, after meeting with the eastern Magyars returned to Hungary in 1239 and informed Béla of the planned Mongol invasion of Europe
2 CONC . Béla wanted to take precautions against the Mongols; therefore he granted asylum, in Hungary, to the Cumans who had been defeated by the Mongols. However, the nomadic culture of the Cumans caused tensions between them and the Hu
2 CONC ngarians which became more and more acute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla tried to reinforce the eastern borders of his kingdom, but the Mongol troops, lead by Batu Khan, managed to break through the frontier defenses on 12 March 1241. On hearing of the Mongols' successful attack, the citizens of P
2 CONC est, who had been accusing the Cumans of cooperating with the Mongols, murdered Köten, the Khan of the Cumans; therefore the enraged Cumans began to plunder the countryside and they left the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mongol invasion of Hungary (tatárjárás)
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Cumans' departure, Béla could lead only a small army against the Mongols who defeated him in the Battle of Mohi on 11 April 1241. After his disastrous defeat, Béla fled to Pozsony and then to Hainburg where Duke Frederic
2 CONC k II of Austria seized his treasury and enforced him to cede three western counties of his kingdom to Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla fled from Hainburg to Zagreb and he sent his envoys to the Emperor Frederick II and Pope Gregory IX to seek their assistance against the Mongols. He even offered to accept the overlordship of the Holy Roman Emperor in case he
2 CONC  sent troops to Hungary, but none of the Western powers provided him any assistance.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, the Mongols were plundering the territories of the kingdom west of the Danube River. Moreover, in January, they could cross the frozen Danube and Béla had to flee from the Mongol troops, the khan sent to capture h
2 CONC im, to Trau.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, in March 1241, the Mongol troops retired unexpectedly from Hungary, but they left a country totally pillaged behind them.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The "Second Founder of our Country"
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the Mongol invasion of Hungary, Béla broke with his former internal policy. Based on the experiences of the occupation, he began to grant estates to his partisans, but simultaneously he also obliged them to build up fort
2 CONC resses there, because only fortresses could resist the conquerors. He also encouraged the towns to protect themselves by erecting walls. He called back the Cumans to Hungary and granted them the deserted territories between the Ri
2 CONC vers Danube and Tisza.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of his successful internal policy, he is greatly respected in Hungary and commonly known as "the second founder" of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]External expansions
2 CONT
2 CONT Already in 1242, he could lead his troops against Duke Frederick II of Austria. During his campaign, he managed to reoccupy Sopron and Kőszeg and he compelled the duke to renounce the three counties he had occupied during the Mong
2 CONC ol invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 June 1244, Béla made a peace with the Republic of Venice and he surrendered his supremacy over Zára but he retained the 1/3 of the Dalmatian city's revenues of customs. In 1245, Béla provided military assistance to his son-i
2 CONC n-law, Prince Rostislav against Prince Danylo of Halych, but the latter forced back the pretender's attacks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his request, Pope Gregory IX absolved Béla of his oath he had taken to the Holy Roman Emperor during the Mongol invasion on 21 August 1245. Shortly afterwards, Duke Frederick II of Austria, who did not give up his claims to t
2 CONC he western counties of the Kingdom of Hungary, launched an attack against Hungary. Although, he could defeate the Hungarian troops in a battle by the Leitha River, but he died in the battle. With his death, the male line of the Ho
2 CONC use of Babenberg became extinct, and a struggle commenced for the rule over Austria and Styria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla granted the Banat of Szörény to the Knights Hospitaller in 1249, when a rumour was spreading that the Mongols were preparing a new campaign against Europe. In the same year, he assisted again his son-in-law against Halych, bu
2 CONC t Prince Danylo defeated his troops by the San River. Finally, Béla decided to make an agreement with the Prince of Halych and they had a meeting in Zólyom in 1250 where Béla promised that he would not assist his son-in-law agains
2 CONC t Prince Danylo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla decided to intervene in the struggle for the inheritance of the House of Babenberg and arranged a marriage between Gertrude of Austria, the niece of the deceased Duke Frederick II of Austria, and Roman Danylovich, a son of Pr
2 CONC ince Danylo of Halych. In 1252, he lead his armies against Austria and occupied the Vienna Basin. However, King Ottokar II of Bohemia, whose wife was Margaret, the sister of Duke Frederick II, also declared his claim to the two du
2 CONC chies. Béla made a campaign against Moravia but he could not occupy Olomouc; therefore he started negotiating with the King of Bohemia with the mediation of the Papal legates. Finally, Béla had a meeting with King Ottokar II in Po
2 CONC zsony and they concluded a peace. Based on the provision of the peace Wiener Neustadt and the Duchy of Styria came under Béla's rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Struggles with his son
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla had had his eldest son, Stephen crowned junior king already in 1246, but he did not want to share the royal power with his son. However, Stephen recruited an army against his father and persuaded Béla to cede him the governme
2 CONC nt of Transylvania in 1258.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, the Styrians, who would have preferred the rule of the King of Bohemia, rose against Béla's reign, but his troops suppressed their rebellion. After his victory, Béla appointed his son to Duke of Styria. Neverthel
2 CONC ess, the Styrians rebelled against the rule of the King of Hungary again with the support of King Otakar II. Béla and his son commenced a military campaign against King Otakar II's lands, but their troops were defeated on July 12,
2 CONC  1260 in the Battle of Kroissenbrunn. Following the battle, Béla renounced his claim to the Duchy of Styria on behalf of the King of Bohemia in the Peace of Pressburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the peace, Stephen took over again the government of Transylvania. Béla and his son lead jointly their armies against Bulgaria in 1261. Nevertheless, Béla favoured his younger son, Duke Béla and his daughter, Anna, t
2 CONC he mother-in-law of the King of Bohemia; therefore his relationship with his elder son was getting tense. The two kings (father and son) began to harass the other's partisans, and their clash seemed inevitable. Finally, the Archbi
2 CONC shops Fülöp of Esztergom and Smaragd of Kalocsa commenced to mediate between them and the two kings signed an agreement in the summer of 1262 in Pozsony. Based on the agreement, Stephen V took over the government of the parts of t
2 CONC he Kingdom East of the Danube.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, their reconciliation was only temporary, because their partisans were continuously inciting them against each other. In 1264, the junior king attached his mother's and sister's estates in his domains. Béla sent troops aga
2 CONC inst his son, whose wife and son were soon captured, while Stephen had to retreat to the Castle of Feketehalom. However, the young king managed to repel the siege of his father's troops and to commence a counter-attack. Stephen V 
2 CONC won a strategic victory over Béla's troops in the Battle of Isaszeg in March 1265 and in the subsequent peace Béla was obliged to cede the government of the Eastern parts of his kingdom again to his son. On March 23, 1266, they co
2 CONC nfirmed personally the peace in the Convent of the Blessed Virgin on the Nyulak szigete ('Rabbits' Island').
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1267, the "prelates and nobles" of the Kingdom of Hungary held a joint assembly in Esztergom, and their decisions were confirmed by both Béla and his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]His last years
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla lost his favourite son in the summer of 1269. Afterwards, his favourite daughter, Anna was exercising more and more influence over him. In his last will, Béla entrusted his daughter and his followers to her son-in-law, King O
2 CONC takar II of Bohemia, because he did not trust his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # around 1218: Maria Laskarina, a daughter of the Emperor Theodore I Lascaris of Nicaea and Anna Angelina
2 CONT
2 CONT Blessed Kinga (5 March 1224 – 24 July 1292), wife of Prince Bolesław V the Chaste of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, (1226/1227 – after 3 July 1271), wife of Prince Rostislav of Slavonia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth, (1236 – 24 October 1271), wife of Henry XIII, Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance, (? – ?), wife of King Leo I of Halych
2 CONT
2 CONT King Stephen V of Hungary, (before 18 October 1239 – 6 August 1272)
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Margaret, (27 January 1242 – 18 January 1271)
2 CONT
2 CONT Blessed Jolenta, (? – 16/17 June after 1303), wife of Duke Boleslaus of Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Béla of Slavonia (c. 1245 – 1269)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla IV (Hungarian: IV. Béla, (1206 – 3 May 1270), King of Hungary and Croatia (1214-1270), Duke of Styria (1254-1258). Béla was present, at the age of seven, when a group of conspirators killed his mother, and he could never forg
2 CONC ive his father's generosity towards the conspirators' accomplices. Shortly afterwards, he was crowned junior king (rex iunior) and he governed several provinces of the Kingdom of Hungary during his father's reign. He endeavoured t
2 CONC o restore the royal power that had declined since the death of his grandfather, King Béla, which resulted in permanent conflicts between Béla and his father. When he ascended the throne, he determined to revive his grandfather's i
2 CONC nternal policy which made him unpopular among his barons. However, he soon had to face the threat of the Mongol invasion of Europe; therefore he granted asylum to the Cumans in order to strengthen his military force. After the Tat
2 CONC ar invasion he became one of the most famous Hungarian kings. Nevertheless, the Mongol armies defeated his troops in a decisive battle requiring his escape to the farthest fortress of his kingdom while the Mongols were pillaging t
2 CONC he country. When the Mongol troops were withdrawn unexpectedly, Béla returned and commenced reconstruction of his devastated kingdom; he patronized towns, constructed new fortresses and encouraged immigration. His success is refle
2 CONC cted by his popular epithet, "the Second Founder of our Country", in Hungary. During the second period of his reign, he proceeded to expand his rule over the neighbouring countries. However his last years were characterized by his
2 CONC  permanent conflicts with his eldest son, Stephen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla was the eldest son of King Andrew II of Hungary and his first wife, Gertrude of Merania. Upon Pope Innocent III's request, the ecclesiastic and temporal dignitaries of the Kingdom of Hungary took an oath before his birth that
2 CONC  they would accept him as his father's successor.
2 CONT
