1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Note: Grosheny, Groshenny, Groshaeny, GROSHEUNY, GROSSHEUNY, GROSSHENNY, Groshenau====
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gressoney-Saint-Jean]
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditionally, the two municipalities of Gressoney-La-Trinité and Gressoney-Saint-Jean form a unique territorial and cultural entity, called Gressoney in French , Greschòney (official) or Creschnau [4] [5] in the local dialect of 
2 CONC the Walser language ( the Greschòneytitsch or more simply Titsch ) and Kressenau [6] (obsolete form [7] ) in German .
2 CONT
2 CONT At the toponym Gressoney have been associated over time various meanings:
2 CONT
2 CONT Chreschen-eye: "plain of the crescioni"
2 CONT Grossen-eys: "great glacier"
2 CONT Chreschen-ey: "egg deposited among the crescioni". The most reliable meaning is the first, although no document attests it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gressoney is divided into three parts [8] :
2 CONT
2 CONT Onderteil , which means "the lower part", from the pont de Trenta (in titsch, Trentostäg ) to the capital of Gressoney-Saint-Jean ( Greschòney Zer Chilchu ); Méttelteil , which means "the middle part", including the capital of Sai
2 CONC nt-Jean ( Greschòney Zer Chilchu ) and the territory upstream to the village of Léschelbalmo; Oberteil , which means "the upper part", which corresponds to the current municipality of Gressoney-La-Trinité . From 1939 to 1946 took 
2 CONC the name - Italianized - of Gressonei San Giovanni , part of the town of Gressonei , then suppressed in the same year 1946.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Ancient Roots:==
2 CONT
2 CONT De Groșeni (or Valea Groșilor) refers to a small river (a right tributary of the Teuz) and the surrounding area/village in western Romania, known for its natural setting with forests, streams, and traditional Romanian life, often 
2 CONC associated with Romanian folklore and rural culture. While primarily a geographical feature, it evokes images of pastoral Romanian landscapes and heritage. 
