2 CONT * '''Vangheluwe, Daniel. 1999.''' '' “Local Communities in Their Landscape in the Rent District of Eersel/Bergeyk (14th-16th Centuries).” In ''Land and Ancestors: Cultural Dynamics in the Urnfield Period and the Middle Ages in the
2 CONC  Southern Netherlands'', edited by Frans Theuws and Nico Roymans. Amsterdam, Netherlands: Amsterdam University Press. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Land_and_Ancestors/kPilyGKrT3cC?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=%22Godfrey%20iii%22%20119
2 CONC 0 Google Books]:—
2 CONT ** “...from 1128 onwards, the counts of Louvain lived through a difficult period: in this year king Lothar III (1125-1137) took away from Godfrey I his ducal dignity and granted it to Walram II, count of Limburg (duke 1128-1139). 
2 CONC The next year Godfrey lost the battle of Wilderen (near Duras) against the bishop of Liège and Walram of Limburg, which considerably decreased his power. His son Godfrey II (1139-1142) recaptured the ducal title, but only survived
2 CONC  his father for three years. He left behind a one-year old son, '''Godfrey III (1142-1190)''', who could only establish his personal authority from 1155 onwards. During his minority his mother, Lutgard of Sulzbach, sister of the w
2 CONC ife of Emperor Conrad III (1138-1152), acted as re-gent. This possibly accounts for the duke's aloofness during Postel's foundation phase and in the other Premonstratensian foundations.”
2 CONT * '''Huffman, Joseph P. 2024.''' “Separation of Imperial and Regional Politics: Lotharingia and Westphalia.” In ''Medieval Cologne: From Rhineland Metropolis to European City (A.D. 1125–1475)'', 54–84. Boston, MA: Walter de Gruyte
2 CONC r. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Medieval_Cologne/Yx0rEQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Godfrey+iii%22+1190&pg=PA64&printsec=frontcover Google Books]:—
2 CONT ** '''Footnote 28''' – Lotharingia’s two duchies (Upper and Lower) “both appear on the ‘''nomina principium imperii''’ as witnesses of an 1165 charter issued by Emperor Friedrich I Barbarossa as "'''Gotafridus Louanie [Louvain] du
2 CONC x'''" and "''Henricus Ardenne dux''," from which the titles duke of Brabant and duke of Limburg respectively are first documented: Heinrich Appelt, ed., ''Die Urkunden der deutschen Könige und Kaiser Friedrich I: 1158-1167'' [MGH 
2 CONC DD 10: 2) [Hanover: Hahn Verlag, 1929; rpt. 1979) 429 no. 500 (29 December 1165 in Aachen). The counts (and then landgraves) of Louvain thus became the dukes of Brabant with '''Godfrey III,''' and Barbarossa confirmed the ducal ti
2 CONC tle for Godfrey Ill's son Henry I in 1183 while Henry served as regent during his father's pilgrimage to Jerusalem (1182-1184), and Barbarossa confirmed the ducal title for Henry I once again at the '''death of Godfrey III in 1190
2 CONC '''….”
2 CONT * '''Flower, William, and Charles Best Norcliffe. (1881) 2024.''' “Section 2.” In ''The Visitation of Yorkshire in the Years 1563 and 1564, Made by William Flower, Esquire, Norroy King of the Arms''. Frankfurt, Deutschland: Verlag
2 CONC . [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Visitation_of_Dorkshire_in_the_Years/sbYIEQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Godfrey+iii%22+1190&pg=PA248&printsec=frontcover Google Books]:—
2 CONT ** Note. – “…Godfrey II., Duke of Lothier, was [Godfrey I.’s] eldest son by his first wife, Ida of Namur, and died in 1143, leaving, but Lutgarde of Sulzbach, Godrey III., who died 10 August 1190, father, by Margaret of Limburg, o
2 CONC f Henry I., Duke of Brabant, whose descendants, in England alone, are countless.”
