1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ida of Lorraine''', born between 1035 and 1040 in Bouillon, Ardennes, Lower Lorraine, was the daughter of Godfrey II the Bearded, Duke of Lorraine, and Doda (some sources suggest her surname was de Rethel van Lo
2 CONC one, while others propose de Perigod, and some claim her surname remains unknown). The Vita B. Idæ Boloniensis Comitissæ records that she was the daughter of “pater…Godefridus, mater…Doda.” It is believed that she descended in the
2 CONC  female line from Charlemagne, whose brother became Pope Stephen IX. Her marriage is documented in Orderic Vitalis, which specifies that she was the sister of “Godfrey Duke of Lotharingia” and names her three sons: Eustace III, Go
2 CONC dfrey of Boulogne, and Baldwin.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221517105845&size=large}}
2 CONT Eustace inherited Boulogne; Godfrey ascended to the title of Godfrey V, Duke of Lorraine, and became one of the nine worthies. Baldwin, on the other hand, became the first king of Jerusalem. Both Godfrey and Baldwin played signifi
2 CONC cant roles as leaders in the First Crusade. Ida’s brother, Godfrey III the Hunchback, was married to Matilda of Tuscany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida of Lorraine is also known by many other names, including Blessed Ida of Louvain, Ida of Lower Lorraine, Ida of Verdun, Ida d’Ardenne, Saint Ida d’Ardennes, Countess de Lorraine, Sainte Ide de Verdun, Ida de Bouillon, Ida von N
2 CONC iederlothringen, and Ida La Tour d’Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that Ida of Lorraine played a pivotal role in facilitating the reconciliation between her husband and William the Conqueror (William of Normandy), and the English throne. According to the Doomsday Book, upon William’s
2 CONC  ascension to the English throne, she held five manors in England directly from the king: Kingweston (Dorset), Bockhampton, Swanage, Winterbourne Monkton (Somerset), and Nutfield (Surrey). From her base around Tongres and her dowr
2 CONC y of Genappe, she provided support to her son Godfrey’s efforts to govern Lorraine in the early 1080s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida of Lorraine maintained correspondence with Saint Anselm of Canterbury, her spiritual advisor, until her death. Renowned for her profound piety, she cultivated a network of ecclesiastical allies. Her spiritual advisor, St. Anse
2 CONC lm, the abbot of Bec at the time, occasionally visited her from Normandy or frequently composed uplifting letters. She maintained a cordial correspondence with Anselm of Canterbury, actively promoted the Cluniac reform movement un
2 CONC der his guidance, and some of his letters to Ida have fortunately survived. She granted land in Boulogne to Bec Abbey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess Ida was a fervent advocate for Cluniac reform. She corresponded with Hugh of Cluny, requesting his assistance in sending Cluniac monks to the newly established monastery at Wast/Vaast. Although the letter is now lost, its
2 CONC  existence is corroborated by an account of Hugh’s positive response: “Ida comitissa” s. Hugoni, abbati, ut mitteret Cluniacensis ecclesiae quosdam fratres ad locum Wast appelatum institutione monastica inornandum supplicavit, atq
2 CONC ue multiplicatis et devotis precibus exoravit quatenus eam in filiam adoptionis redimeret. Sanctus … Hugo haec audiens … desiderio atque petitione Dominae devote satisfecit.”
2 CONT '''English Translation:'''
2 CONT “Countess Ida, in a fervent plea, implored St. Hugh, the esteemed abbot, to dispatch certain brothers of the Cluniac order to establish a monastic institution in the region of Wast. Her heartfelt prayers and unwavering devotion we
2 CONC re instrumental in securing his approval to adopt her as his cherished daughter. Saint Hugh, upon hearing her earnest request, was deeply moved and fulfilled her desire and petition with utmost satisfaction.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida, who generously donated a significant portion of her considerable wealth to charitable causes, was also known for her exceptional craftsmanship in creating exquisite ornaments for altars. Following the demise of her husband in
2 CONC  1093, she made a decisive decision to sell all her disposable possessions and dedicate them to the establishment and endowment of religious institutions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout her life, Ida founded several monasteries, including:
2 CONT - Saint-Wulmer in Samer
2 CONT - Boulogne-sur-Mer for the Augustinian Canons
2 CONT - Our Lady of the Chapel in Calais
2 CONT - The Abbey of Saint-Bertin in Saint-Omer, France
2 CONT - Abbey of Cappelle in Marck
2 CONT - The Abbey of Saint-Vaast at Saint-Michel-du-Wast for the Cluniac Monks in Arras
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her monastic foundation efforts, Ida made substantial donations to the Abbeys of Lens, Saint-Bertin, Bouillon, and Afflighem, as well as the Abbey of Munsterbilzen, where she had received her education.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida did not take the Benedictine Habit, as has been believed, but she did obtain it from St. Ugo at Cluny. Current scholarship suggests that she did not actually become a Benedictine Nun but rather a “Secular Oblate of the Benedic
2 CONC tine Order.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lamberti Audomariensis Chronica records her death as April 1113 (in La Capelle, according to Horst Van Cuyck in a self-authored work). Her feast was originally celebrated in the ancient Diocese of Boulogne, later authorized in
2 CONC  the Diocese of Arras and Bayeux. Blessed Ida of Louvain is also remembered on April 13, 1113, although the date of her death, beyond April of 1113 is unknown.  (Many sources suggest April 13, 1113.  Douglas Richardson in his work
2 CONC  Royal Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families, 5 vols, ed. Kimball G. Everingham (Salt Lake City: the author, 2013), volume I, page 465 suggests August 13, 1113.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditionally, her burial place has been ascribed to the Monastery of Saint Vaast. Her relics were transferred to the Bénédictines de l’Adoration perpétuelle du Saint-Sacrement in Paris and again in 1808 to her final resting place
2 CONC  at the Cathédrale Notre-Dame de Bayeux aka Our Lady of Bayeux, Calvados, Normandy when the Benedictines moved there. However, one relic, reportedly a rib, was left in Wast.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is venerated today in Bayeux.
2 CONT
2 CONT
