2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida of Lorraine (also referred to as Blessed Ida of Boulogne) (c. 1040 – 13 Apr 1113) was a saint and noblewoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Bouillon, Ardenne, South Belgium, the daughter of Godfrey III, Duke of Lower Lorraine and his wife, Doda.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, she married Eustace II of Boulogne. They had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eustace III, the next Count of Boulogne 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godfrey of Bouillon, first ruler of Kingdom of Jerusalem 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin, second ruler of Kingdom of Jerusalem 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida shunned the use of a wet-nurse in raising her sons. Instead, she breast-fed them to insure that they were not contaminated by the wet-nurse's morals. When her sons went on the First Crusade, Ida contributed heavily to their ex
2 CONC penses.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ida of Lorraine (also referred to as Blessed Ida of Boulogne) (c. 1040 – 13 Apr 1113) was a saint and noblewoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Bouillon, Ardenne, South Belgium, the daughter of Godfrey III, Duke of Lower Lorraine and his wife, Doda.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, she married Eustace II of Boulogne. They had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eustace III, the next Count of Boulogne 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godfrey of Bouillon, first ruler of Kingdom of Jerusalem 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin, second ruler of Kingdom of Jerusalem 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida shunned the use of a wet-nurse in raising her sons. Instead, she breast-fed them to insure that they were not contaminated by the wet-nurse's morals. When her sons went on the First Crusade, Ida contributed heavily to their ex
2 CONC penses.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
