1 OCCU Landgreve i Thüringen 1217-1227
1 TITL Landgraf
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Saint Louis IV or Louis of Thuringia ( October 28 , 1200 in Creuzburg – September 11 , 1227 in Otranto ) was Landgrave of Thuringia and Count Palatine of Saxony from 1217 to 1227. The heyday of Ludowingian rule be
2 CONC gan under his rule Thuringia continued, at the same time their decline was initiated by his early death. He was married to Saint Elizabeth of Thuringia .
2 CONT life 
2 CONT Ludwig was born in 1200 as the second of four sons of Landgrave Hermann I at Creuzburg Castle in the town of the same name on the Werra .
2 CONT After the death of his father in 1217, Ludwig took over the government. He soon got into serious conflicts with the Archbishop of Mainz , which were settled by Emperor Frederick II .
2 CONT Since 1221, Louis was married to Elisabeth , daughter of the Hungarian King Andreas II . Elisabeth came to the Wartburg as a bride for Ludwig's deceased brother Hermann in 1211 and spent almost her entire childhood at the Landgrav
2 CONC e's court. She later went down in history as Saint Elizabeth.
2 CONT In 1221, Ludwig's brother-in-law, Margrave Dietrich von Meißen, died . Louis received guardianship over his nephew, Henry the Illustrious . This seemed to offer the possibility of territorial expansion. Ludwig used military force 
2 CONC to advance as far as Lower Lusatia , but encountered resistance from his sister Jutta , Heinrich's mother. Ludwig IV maintained good relations with Emperor Friedrich II, who appointed Ludwig to the rank of marshal and from whom he
2 CONC  obtained the eventual enfeoffment of the margraviate of Meissen in 1226 . In return, Ludwig joined the crusade of Frederick II.to Jerusalem .
2 CONT Gravestone for Landgrave Ludwig IV, since 1952 in the Georgenkirche in Eisenach
2 CONT On June 24, 1227, Ludwig left Creuzburg with his army . He crossed the Alps and joined the main imperial army in southern Italy with his contingent. However, before the journey by sea to the Kingdom of Jerusalem could be continued
2 CONC , he died of a fever in the camp near Otranto . Elisabeth received the news of Ludwig's death in October 1227. He was buried in Reinhardsbrunn Monastery , the house monastery of the Ludowinger family . The Reinhardsbrunn figure gr
2 CONC ave slab with his portrait, which, however, was created posthumously (after the monastery fire of 1292) and can be dated to after 1350 based on the representation, has been placed in the Georgenkirche in Eisenach since 1952 .
2 CONT Ludwig's successor as Landgrave of Thuringia officially became his five-year-old son Hermann II , under the guardianship and regency of his brother Heinrich Raspe .
2 CONT Marriage and Descendants 
2 CONT Louis IV married Elizabeth of Hungary in 1221 . With her he had three children:
2 CONT Hermann II (1222–1241) ⚭ Helene of Brunswick-Lüneburg (1239)
2 CONT Sophie (1224–1275) ⚭ Henry II , Duke of Brabant
2 CONT Gertrud (1227–1297) abbess in the Premonstratensian monastery in Altenberg (Hesse)
2 CONT Worship of Saints 
2 CONT While the veneration of Ludwig's wife Elisabeth spread rapidly and was encouraged by the church, the veneration of Ludwig remained limited to Thuringia and died out in the 14th century without the church canonizing her."
2 CONT [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludwig_IV._(Thüringen)]
