1 OCCU Crusader, COUNT OF GUELDERS AND ZUTPHEN, graaf van GELRE en ZUTPHEN, comte de Gueldre/Zutphen, Comte, de Gueldres, Greve i Geldern 1182-1207, graaf van Gelre
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =OTTO I Graaf van Gelre en Zütphen.=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Heinrich II and Agnes of Arnstein.
2 CONT * Noble family: House of Wassenberg
2 CONT * Otto I of Guelders (1150–1207) was a Count of Guelders and Zutphen from 1182 until his death in 1207. He was a son of Duke Hendrik of Guelders and Agnes of Arnstein. He married Richardis of Bavaria in 1184. Richardis was a daugh
2 CONC ter of Otto I Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria.
2 CONT * Otto I of Guelders (1150–1207) was a Count of Guelders and Zutphen from 1182 until his death in 1207.
2 CONT * Otto I of Gelre, born around 1150, died on August 25, 1207, buried at Klooster Kamp in Kamp-Lintfort) was Count of Gelre (1182-1207). He was a son of Count Hendrik van Gelre and Zutphen and Agnes from an unknown family.  Around 
2 CONC 1184 Otto married Richarda of Bavaria, a daughter of Count Palatine Otto I of Bavaria. He took part in the Third Crusade (1189-1192) and took part in the siege of Konya and the siege of Akko. Otto was one of the last nobles from t
2 CONC he Low Countries to return from the Crusade. Otto sided with the emperor and against the counts of Holland and the dukes of Brabant. He supported Louis II van Loon during the Loon War and in 1190 he was named the first count of Ge
2 CONC lre and Zutphen. The relevant certificate is the city letter to Zutphen. Otto was the first city in Gelderland to grant Zutphen city rights. His son Gerard III and grandson Otto II would use this Zutphen right as a model for the c
2 CONC ity privileges of various other Gelderland cities, making Zutphen the mother city of the Gelderland county.
2 CONT *[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/140284734/otto_i-von_geldern '''Otto I von Geldern - Find A Grave Memorial''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Richardis of Bavaria in 1184.''' Richardis was a daughter of Otto I Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria and Agnes of Loon. Richardis married Count Otto I of Guelders, son of Count Henry (Hendrick) I of Guelders and Agnes of
2 CONC  Arnstein, in 1184.3251 (Count Otto I of Guelders was born in 1150 3251 and died in 1207 
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto and his wife Richardis had 5 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Henry ,''' (?–1198). Was betrothed to Aleidis of Holland in 1197, the daughter of Dirk VII, Count of Holland. Henry died in 1198, before the wedding could take place.
2 CONT * '''Gerard III, Count of Guelders,''' (1185–1229).
2 CONT * '''Adelaide of Guelders,''' (?–1218), married William I, Count of Holland.[3]
2 CONT * '''Otto,''' (1193–1213), was Bishop of Utrecht
2 CONT * '''Irmgard of Guelders,''' married Adolf, Count of Altena and Mark, son of Frederick I, Count of Berg-Altena and his wife Alveradis.
2 CONT * '''Margaret of Guelders,''' married Lothar III, Count of Hochstadt
2 CONT * '''Matilda of Guelders,''' married Henry II, Count of Nassau.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HOLLAND.htm#GerhardIGelderndied1138B Project MedLands HOLLAND]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''OTTO''' (-22 Oct 1207, bur Kloster Kamp).  The Kronik van Arent toe Bocop names "Gherrit und…Otte" as the two sons of "Henderick…grave van Gelre"[1025].  '''He succeeded in 1182 as OTTO I Graaf van Gelre en Zütphen.'''  "Otto…c
2 CONC omes Gelrensis…cum uxore nostra Richarda" granted customs privileges to Kloster Altenburg by charter dated to [1188][1026].  “Otto...Gelre comes” donated property to Bedbur, with the consent of “Richardis uxoris mee et heredum meo
2 CONC rum...Gerardi, Ottonis sive Lodevici”, by charter dated 1203, witnessed by “...Theodericus frater comitis...”[1027].  '''married  ([1185]) RICHARDIS of Bavaria,''' daughter of OTTO I Graf von Wittelsbach Duke of Bavaria & his wife
2 CONC  Agnes van Looz (-Roermond 21 Sep 1231, bur Roermond).  The Genealogia Ottonis II Ducis Bavariæ refers to, but does not name, the fourth of the five daughters of "Otto dictus de Schiren…dux Bawarie" as wife of "Otto comes de Gelre
2 CONC ", specifying that they had three sons[1028].  The Kronik van Arent toe Bocop records that "Otto…grave van Gelre" married "des graven docter van Gullick…Richgerda"[1029], which misstates her origin.  Her name is further confirmed 
2 CONC by the charter dated 1250 under which her grandson "Otto…comes Gelrensis" names "avia mea domina Richardis…avi mei Ottonis comitis"[1030].  "Otto…comes Gelrensis…cum uxore nostra Richarda" granted customs privileges to Kloster Alt
2 CONC enburg by charter dated to [1188][1031].  “Otto...Gelre comes” donated property to Bedbur, with the consent of “Richardis uxoris mee et heredum meorum...Gerardi, Ottonis sive Lodevici”, by charter dated 1203[1032].  “Gerhardus com
2 CONC es Gelrie et Zutphanie” donated property to the church of Zutphen St Walburgis, for the soul of “patris mei Ottonis”, with the consent of “matris mee Richardis et fratrum meorum Ottonis, Xanctensis prepositi, et Lodewici”, by char
2 CONC ter dated 1207[1033].  Abbess of the Munster Abbey at Roermond.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Otto I & his wife RICHARDIS of Bavaria had eight children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>* a)        '''HENDRIK''' (-[1198], bur Rijnsburg).  The Kronik van Arent toe Bocop names "Henderick" as the son of "Otto…grave van Gelre" and his wife[1034].  '''Betrothed (1198) to ALEIDIS [Adelheid] of Holland, daughter of D
2 CONC IRK VII Count of Holland & his wife Adelheid von Kleve ([1186]-before 1203, bur Rijnsburg).'''  The Chronologia Johannes de Beke names "Adelheidim et Adam" as the two daughters of Count Dirk VII & his wife, specifying that Adelhei
2 CONC d was betrothed to "Henrico domicello Gelrie"[1035].  The Annales Egmundani record the betrothal in 1197 of "Theodericus comes filiam Aleydem" and "filio comitis Ottonis…Heinricus" but specifying that "idem puer" died and was buri
2 CONC ed at Rijnsburg[1036].  The Chronologia Johannes de Beke records that "Otto comes Gelrensis" and "Theodericus Hollandensis" were reconciled and agreed the betrothal of "filiam suam Aleidem" and "filio comitis Ottonis…Henricus", bu
2 CONC t specifying in a later passage that both died soon after and were buried "in Rinesburgensi monasterio"[1037]. 
2 CONT >>>* b)         '''GERHARD''' ([1185]-killed in battle 22 Oct 1229, bur Roermond Cistercian Abbey).  The Kronik van Arent toe Bocop names "Gherrit" as the third son of "Otto…grave van Gelre" and his wife[1038].  He succeeded as GE
2 CONC RHARD III Graaf van Gelre. 
2 CONT >>>* c)         '''ADELHEID''' (-4 Feb 1218, bur Rijnsburg).  The Annales Egmundani record the marriage in 1197 of "Wilhelmum fratrem Theoderici comitis" and "filiam comitis Ottonis [Pictavis Aquisgrani]"[1039].  The Chronologia J
2 CONC ohannes de Beke records the marriage of "Wilhelmum comitem" and "Otto comes…Adelheidem suam filiam", specifying that it was celebrated "in Stavria"[1040].  The Gesta Epsicoporum Traiectensium records the marriage of "Wilhelmum" an
2 CONC d "Otto comes Gelrensis…filiam"[1041].  The Chronologia Johannes de Beke records the death "1219 II Id Feb" of "comitissa" and her burial at Rijnsburg[1042].  '''married  (Stavoren, Friesland 1197) as his first wife, WILLEM of Hol
2 CONC land, son of FLORIS III Count of Holland & his wife Ada of Scotland (-4 Feb 1222).'''  He succeeded in 1203 as WILLEM I Count of Holland. 
2 CONT >>>* d)         '''OTTO''' (-Vorthusen Mar 1213, bur Utrecht cathedral).  The Kronik van Arent toe Bocop names "Otto…byscop van Uttert" as the second son of "Otto…grave van Gelre" and his wife[1043].  “Otto...Gelre comes” donated 
2 CONC property to Bedbur, with the consent of “Richardis uxoris mee et heredum meorum...Gerardi, Ottonis sive Lodevici”, by charter dated 1203[1044].  Provost of Xanten.  “Gerhardus comes Gelrie et Zutphanie” donated property to the chu
2 CONC rch of Zutphen St Walburgis, for the soul of “patris mei Ottonis”, with the consent of “matris mee Richardis et fratrum meorum Ottonis, Xanctensis prepositi, et Lodewici”, by charter dated 1207[1045].  Bishop of Utrecht 1213.  The
2 CONC  Chronologia Johannes de Beke records that "dominus Adulfus Coloniensis, Otto Monasteriensis et Gerardus Osnaburgensis fratres et pontifices, Wilhelmus Hollandie ac Otto Gelrie comites" met in Utrecht and elected "Otto Xanctensis 
2 CONC prepositus…frater [error for filius] eiusdem comitis Gelrie et sororius prefati comitis Hollandie…iuvenis" as bishop of Utrecht, but that he died "1205 apud Vorthusen febre pulsatus VII Kal Apr" and was buried at Utrecht[1046].  T
2 CONC he Kronik van Arent toe Bocop records that "Otto van Ghelre" was installed as thirty-third bishop of Utrecht in 1210, although he was no older than eighteen years old, died in 1213 and was buried in Utrecht cathedral[1047]. 
2 CONT >>>* e)         '''LUDWIG''' (-after 1207).  “Otto...Gelre comes” donated property to Bedbur, with the consent of “Richardis uxoris mee et heredum meorum...Gerardi, Ottonis sive Lodevici”, by charter dated 1203[1048].  “Gerhardus 
2 CONC comes Gelrie et Zutphanie” donated property to the church of Zutphen St Walburgis, for the soul of “patris mei Ottonis”, with the consent of “matris mee Richardis et fratrum meorum Ottonis, Xanctensis prepositi, et Lodewici”, by c
2 CONC harter dated 1207[1049]. 
2 CONT >>>* f)          '''IRMGARD''' (-after 1230).  Levoldus’s mid-14th century Chronica Comitum de Marka records that “comes Adulphus” married “sorore comitis Gelrie”[1050].  “Otto...comes in Dale” donated “domum in Eklo” to Kloster F
2 CONC lasheim, with the consent of “Richarde uxore nostra et eius matre comitissa de Altena domina Ermigarde”, by charter dated 1225[1051].  '''married  (after 1210) as his second wife, ADOLF [I] Graf von der Mark, son of FRIEDRICH [I] 
2 CONC Graf von Altena & his wife Alveradis --- (-28 Jun 1249).'''
2 CONT >>>* g)         '''MARGARETA''' (-after 1264).  "Lutharius…comes de Hostadin filius Lutharii comitis, et Margaretha uxor eius" recognised that the churches of "Vremesdorp et de Rummerskirchen" belonged to Knechtsteden abbey, by ch
2 CONC arter dated Feb 1227[1052].  “Lotharius comes de Hostade” settled a dispute with Kloster Schillingscapellen by charter dated Jul 1237, witnessed by "Margareta comitissa, Theodericus et Gerardus filii mei…"[1053].  "Margareta comit
2 CONC issa Hostadensis, Th. comes Hostadensis et Gerardus filii mei" granted rights in Hengebach castle to Wilhelm Graf von Jülich by charter dated 2 Nov 1242[1054].  '''married (before 1216) LOTHAR [III] Graf von Hochstaden, son of LOT
2 CONC HAR [I] Graf von Hochstaden & his wife Mechtild von Vianden (-[1237/44]).'''
2 CONT >>>* h)         '''MECHTILD''' (-28 Oct after 1247).  "Heinricus et Roppertus comites de Nassovva" bought property from Mainz cathedral, with the consent of "uxorum nostrarum Methildis et Gertrudis", by charter dated 11 Dec 1215[1
2 CONC 055].  "Heinricus…comes de Nassowe…collateralis nostra…Methildis" confirmed the dependence of "ecclesie in Nepphe" to Kloster Arnstein by charter dated 9 Jun 1239[1056].  "Henricus comes de Nassowe et Methildis comitissa uxor eius
2 CONC dem, Walramus primogenitus, ceterique liberi eorundem" donated tolls from a bridge to Altenburg abbey by charter dated 20 Jul 1245[1057].  "Heinricus comes de Nassowe et Mectildis comitissa" donated "ecclesiam in Inferi[ori] Diffe
2 CONC nbach" to Kloster Arnstein by charter dated 1247, witnessed by "…filiorum nostrorum Walerami, Ottonis, Henrici, Gerardi, Johannis"[1058].  The necrology of Arnstein an der Lahn records the death 28 Oct of "Mecktildis comitisse de 
2 CONC Nassauw sororis nostre"[1059].  '''married  (before 1221) HEINRICH [II] Graf von Nassau, son of WALRAM [I] von Laurenburg Graf von Nassau &  his wife Kunigunde --- (1180-before 25 Jan 1251).''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_I,_Count_of_Guelders Otto I of Guelders (1150–1207)]
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto I of Guelders joined his Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa on the Third Crusade (1189–1192) during which he aided the Crusader army in the taking of Iconium. After the death of Frederick I Barbarossa, some of the crusaders in th
2 CONC e army left for home but Otto joined one of the groups that went on towards Syria and Palestine. After arriving in the Holy Land, Otto joined the army of Guy of Lusignan, the King of Jerusalem, who was besieging the city of Acre. 
2 CONC Further hardships decimated the army of the late Frederick, and by the spring of 1191 most of them had left for home. Otto was the only survivor from the Low Countries, and returned home in 1190.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned as the first Count of the combined area of Guelders and Zutphen in 1190. One of his sons was Otto, Bishop of Utrecht from 1212 until 1213.
2 CONT
