1 OCCU Herzog in Bayern 1231-1253, , Pfalzgraf bei Rhein 1227-1253
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Otto II of Bavaria (German: Otto II der Erlauchte, Herzog von Bayern, Pfalzgraf bei Rhein, 7 April 1206 in Kelheim – 29 November 1253) known as Otto the Illustrious was the Duke of Bavaria and Count Palatine of the
2 CONC  Rhine (see Electorate of the Palatinate). He was the son of Louis I and Ludmilla of Bohemia and a member of the Wittelsbach dynasty.
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto was born at Kelheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of sixteen, he was married to Agnes of the Palatinate, a granddaughter of Duke Henry the Lion and Conrad of Hohenstaufen. With this marriage, the Wittelsbach inherited the Palatinate and kept it as a Wittelsbach possess
2 CONC ion until 1918. Since that time also the lion has become a heraldic symbol in the coat of arms for Bavaria and the Palatinate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto acquired the rich regions of Bogen in 1240, and Andechs and Ortenburg in 1248 as possessions for the Wittelsbach and extended his power base in Bavaria this way. With the county of Bogen, the Wittelsbach acquired also the whi
2 CONC te and blue coloured lozenge flag which since that time has been the flag of Bavaria (and of the Palatinate).
2 CONT
2 CONT After a dispute with emperor Frederick II was ended, he joined the Hohenstaufen party in 1241. His daughter, Elizabeth, was married to Frederick's son Conrad IV. Because of this, Otto was excommunicated by the pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Landshut in 1253. Like his forefathers, Otto was buried in the crypt of Scheyern Abbey.
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto married Agnes, the daughter of Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine (a son of Henry the Lion) and Agnes of Hohenstaufen, in Worms in 1222. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Louis II, Duke of Bavaria (13 April 1229, Heidelberg – 2 February 1294, Heidelberg).
2 CONT     Henry XIII, Duke of Bavaria (19 November 1235, Landshut – 3 February 1290, Burghausen.
2 CONT     Elisabeth of Bavaria, Queen of Germany (c. 1227, Landshut – 9 October 1273)
2 CONT     Sophie (1236, Landshut – 9 August 1289, Castle Hirschberg), married 1258 to Count Gerhard IV of Sulzbach and Hirschberg.
2 CONT     Agnes (c. 1240–c. 1306), Nun in Segenstal Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT     Arnold, Benjamin (1985). German Knighthood 1050 - 1300. Oxford: Clarendon Press. ISBN 0-19-821960-1.
2 CONT     Arnold, Benjamin (1991). Count and Bishop in Medieval Germany: A Study of Regional Power, 1100 - 1350. Pennsylvania: University of Pennsylvania Press.
2 CONT     Arnold, Benjamin (1991). Princes and territories in medieval Germany. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 0-521-39085-0.
2 CONT     Bachrach, David S. (2016). The Histories of a Medieval German City, Worms c. 1000-c.1300. New York: Routledge. ISBN 9781472436412.
2 CONT     Holzfurtner, Ludwig (2005). Die Wittelsbacher: Staat und Dynastie in acht Jahrhunderten (Urban-Taschenbucher). Kohlhammer Verlag. ISBN 978-3170181915.
2 CONT     Hubensteiner, Benno (2013). Bayerische Geschichte. Munich: Rosenheimer Verlagshaus. ISBN 978-3475537561.
2 CONT     Stevens, John (1706). The History of Bavaria: From the First Ages, to This Present Year.
2 CONT     Peltzer, Jörg (2013). Die Wittelsbacher und die Kurpfalz im Mittelalter: Eine Erfolgsgeschichte?. Schnell & Steiner. ISBN 978-3795426453.
2 CONT     Reiss-Engelhorn-Museen, Mannheim (2013). Die Wittelsbacher am Rhein. Die Kurpfalz und Europa: 2 Bände. Schnell & Steiner. ISBN 978-3795426446.
2 CONT     Schmid, Gregor M. (2014). Die Familie, die Bayern erfand: Das Haus Wittelsbach: Geschichten, Traditionen, Schicksale, Skandale. Munich: Stiebner. ISBN 978-3830710608.
2 CONT     Vogel, Susanne (2012). Die Wittelsbacher: Herzöge - Kurfürsten - Könige in Bayern von 1180 bis 1918. Biografische Skizzen. Staackmann. ISBN 978-3886752485
