1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BOHEMIA.htm#WenzelIdied1253B Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://finnholbek.dk/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I27108&tree=2
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: Wenzel war der Sohn von König Ottokar I. Přemysl von Böhmen und Konstanze, Tochter von Béla III., König von Ungarn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sein Vater ließ den Sohn bereits 1228 zum König krönen. Nach Schilderungen des Historikers Palacký (s.u.) war er ein begeisterter Jäger, begabt und energisch, auf der anderen Seite aber auch dem luxuriösen Leben zugetan. Auf einer
2 CONC  Jagd verlor er ein Auge. Dies brachte ihm den Spitznamen der Einäugige ein.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nach seiner Einsetzung als König führte er die Politik der Premysliden fort. Er förderte die Ansiedlung von Deutschen, um die Landwirtschaft zu modernisieren und dem Bergbau Impulse zu geben. Darüber hinaus begann unter seiner Her
2 CONC rschaft eine Welle von Stadt- und Klostergründungen, vor allem des Zisterzienserordens. Er gründete unter anderem die Städte Trautenau, Schlan und Mies. Gefördert durch seinen wachsenden Reichtum veranstaltete er Ritterturniere, d
2 CONC ie sein deutscher Ritter Holger von Friedberg organisierte. Am königlichen Hof hielten sich zahlreiche Minnesänger auf, angeblich soll dort auch Walther von der Vogelweide aufgetreten sein.
2 CONT
2 CONT Als eine seiner ersten staatsmännischen Aufgaben beendete er den Streit mit dem letzten österreichischen Babenberger, dem Herzog Friedrich. Dieser verstieß seine Ehefrau Sophie, Tochter des ungarischen Königs, wegen Kinderlosigkei
2 CONC t. Wenzels Mutter, die ebenfalls aus Ungarn stammte, empfand dies als Kränkung, und Wenzel nahm dies zum Anlass, um 1230 in Österreich einzufallen. Friedrich wiederum nutzte den Zwist zwischen Wenzel und seinem jüngeren Bruder Pře
2 CONC mysl von Mähren und fiel in Mähren ein. Er besetzte dabei die damals als uneinnehmbar geltende Burg Bítov. Wenzel, der sich mit seiner Armee in der Nähe befand, griff dabei nicht ein. Als Friedrich erkrankte und sich nach Österrei
2 CONC ch zurückzog, nutzte Wenzel die Gunst der Stunde und eroberte Brünn. Der sich verschärfende Zwist führte schließlich dazu, dass Kaiser Friedrich II. über den Österreicher den kaiserlichen Bann verhängte und Wenzel beauftragte, ihn
2 CONC  zum Gehorsam zu bewegen. Wenzel eroberte Niederösterreich und Wien, Friedrich zog sich nach Wiener Neustadt zurück. Die Sorge der Reichsfürsten, dass die wachsende Macht des Kaisers die ihrige schwächen könnte, führte zu politisc
2 CONC hen Begegnungen, die schließlich in einem Frieden zwischen Wenzel und dem österreichischen Herzog mündete. Friedrich verlobte seine Nichte Gertrud mit Wenzels Sohn Vladislav. Als Mitgift erhielt der Böhme die Verwaltung Österreich
2 CONC s nördlich der Donau.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1241 fielen die Mongolen unter Batu Khan nach der Schlacht bei Wahlstatt in Mähren ein. Wenzel konnte sie zwar vertreiben, dennoch waren die Schäden durch den Einfall groß. 1242 wurde Wenzel zusammen mit Heinrich Raspe von Friedri
2 CONC ch II. zum Reichsgubernator für seinen minderjährigen Sohn Konrad IV. bestellt. So sollte ein drohender Krieg unter den rivalisierenden deutschen Fürsten verhindert werden. Seit Ende 1247 opponierte sein Sohn Ottokar II.; unterstü
2 CONC tzt von mährischen Adeligen, erhob er sich endgültig 1248 gegen seinen Vater, der zu dieser Zeit kaum noch regierte, sondern sich mehr Festen widmete. Die Kriege zwischen Sohn und Vater, der Anhänger in Deutschland und Österreich 
2 CONC suchte, dauerten das ganze Jahr – mit wechselnden Erfolgen. Der Vater musste Prag verlassen und begab sich in das Herrschaftsgebiet seines Anhängers Boresch IV. von Riesenburg. Nach dem Sieg Wenzels bei Brüx ließ er seinen Sohn in
2 CONC haftieren und dieser musste sich ihm unterwerfen. Nachkommen [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenzel I. war verheiratet mit Kunigunde von Schwaben. Folgende Nachkommen aus dieser Ehe sind bekannt:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Vladislav († 3. Januar 1247), Markgraf von Mähren, 1246/47 Anwärter auf die Herzogtümer Österreich und Steiermark
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ 1246 Gertrud von Österreich.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ottokar II. (1232–1278), König von Böhmen
2 CONT     * Beatrix (Božena) († 25. Mai 1286) ∞ 1243 Markgraf Otto III., der Fromme von Brandenburg
2 CONT     * Agnes († 1268) ∞ 1244 Markgraf Heinrich III., der Erlauchte von Meissen
2 CONT     * (Tochter)
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatur [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Josef Žemlička: Století posledních Přemyslovců Prag, 1986
2 CONT     * František Palacký: Geschichte von Böhmen
2 CONT     * Wilhelm Wegener: Die Přemysliden
2 CONT     * Georg Juritsch: Beiträge zur böhmischen Geschichte in der Zeit der Přemysliden
2 CONT     * Adolf Zycha: Über den Ursprung der Städte in Böhmen und die Städtepolitik der Prěmysliden
2 CONT
2 CONT Weblinks [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * genealogie-mittelalter.de
2 CONT     * Kurzvita auf der offiziellen Seite von Brünn
2 CONT
2 CONT Einzelnachweise [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ↑ František Teplý: Dějiny města Jindřichova Hradce. Dílu I. svazek 1., Jindřichův Hradec 1927, S. 56
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Wenceslaus I of Bohemia From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus I Premyslid (Czech Václav I.) (c. 1205 – September 23, 1253) was King of Bohemia from 1230 to 1253.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus was a son of Ottokar I of Bohemia and his second wife Constance of Hungary. He was a younger, paternal half-brother to Margarethe of Bohemia and Božislava of Bohemia. His half-sisters were respectively the first Queen c
2 CONC onsort of Valdemar II of Denmark and the first wife of Henry I, Count of Ortenburg. His sister Judith of Bohemia was married to Bernhard von Spanheim, Duke of Carinthia.His sister Anna of Bohemia was married to Henry II the Pious,
2 CONC  Duke of Wrocław. His sister Agnes of Bohemia was Mother Superior of the Franciscan Poor Clares nuns of Prague. In 1989, Agnes was canonized as a saint by Pope John Paul II. [1] His maternal grandparents were Béla III of Hungary a
2 CONC nd his second wife Agnes of Antioch (Agnes de Châtillon). [2] Agnes was a daughter of Raynald of Chatillon and Constance of Antioch (joint princes of Antioch). [3] [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1224, Wenceslaus married Kunigunde of Hohenstaufen, second daughter of Philip of Swabia, King of Germany and his wife, Irene Angelina. [4] Her paternal grandparents were Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor and Beatrice I, Countess 
2 CONC of Burgundy. [5] Her maternal grandparents were Isaac II Angelos, Byzantine Emperor and his first wife Herina. [6]Wenceslaus encouraged large numbers of Germans to settle in the villages and towns in Bohemia and Moravia. Stone bui
2 CONC ldings began to replace wooden ones in Prague as a result of the influence of the new settlers. Wenceslaus and Kunigunde had five known children[7]: Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia (c. 1228 - 3 January 1247). Ottokar II of Bohemia
2 CONC  (c. 1230 - 26 August 1278). Beatrix of Bohemia (c. 1231 - 27 May 1290). Married Otto III, Margrave of Brandenburg. Agnes of Bohemia (d. 10 August 1268). Married Henry III, Margrave of Meissen. An unnamed daughter. Died young. [ed
2 CONC it]Early reign
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 February 1228, Wenceslaus was crowned as co-ruler of the Kingdom of Bohemia with his father. On 15 December 1230, Ottokar died and Wenceslaus succeeded him as the senior King of Bohemia. [8] His early reign was preoccupied by
2 CONC  the threat to Bohemia posed by Frederick II, Duke of Austria. The expansionism of Frederick caused the concern and protestation of several other rulers. In 1236, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor was involved in a war against the 
2 CONC Lombard League. The Emperor demanded Wenceslaus and other rulers of the Holy Roman Empire to lend him part of their own troops for his war effort. Wenceslaus led a group of princes who expressed their reluctance to divert any troo
2 CONC ps from the defense of their own territories, citing fear of invasion from the Duchy of Austria. They requested imperial intervention in the situation. [9] [10] In June, 1236, the Emperor imposed an imperial ban on the Duke of Aus
2 CONC tria. Troops dispatched against the Duke, forced him to flee Vienna for Wiener Neustadt. He would continue to rule a rump state for the following year. The Emperor declared direct imperial rule in both Austria and the Duchy of Sty
2 CONC ria, also held by the fleeing Duke. Ekbert von Andechs-Meranien, former Bishop of Bamberg was installed as governor in the two Duchies. [11] Ekbert would govern from February to his death on 5 June 1237. [12] Wenceslaus was hardly
2 CONC  pleased with this apparent expansion of direct imperial authority close to his borders. Wenceslaus and Duke Frederick formed an alliance against the Emperor. Frederick the Emperor chose to lift the ban in 1237 rather than maintai
2 CONC n another open front. Wenceslaus managed to negotiate the expansion of Bohemia north of the Danube, annexing territories offered by Duke Frederick in order of forming and maintaining their alliance. [13] [14] Wenceslaus and Freder
2 CONC ick also found another ally in the person of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria. In June, 1239, Wenceslaus and Otto left the Reichstag at Eger, abandoning the service of excommunicated Emperor Frederick II. Despite their intent 
2 CONC to elect an antiking[15] [16] no such election would take place until 1246. In 1246, Henry Raspe, Landgrave of Thuringia was elected King of Germany in opposition to Emperor Frederick II and Conrad IV of Germany. [17] [edit]Mongol
2 CONC  invasion
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1241 Wenceslaus successfully repelled a raid on Bohemia by forces serving under Batu Khan and Subutai of the Mongol Empire as part of the Mongol invasion of Europe. The Mongols did not sent their main army to the Kingdom of Pol
2 CONC and, Bohemia and Silesia and only Moravia suffered devastation at their hands. The raids into these four areas were led by Baidar, Kadan and Orda Khan with a force of around 20,000 Mongols. Following the Mongol victory at the Batt
2 CONC le of Legnica, Wenceslaus fell back to gather reinforcements from Thuringia and Saxony, but was overtaken by the Mongol vanguard at Kłodzko. However, the Bohemian cavalry easily fended off the Mongol detachment. As Baidar and Kada
2 CONC n's orders had been to serve as a diversion, they turned away from Bohemia and Poland and went southward to join Batu and Subutai, who had crushed the Hungarians at the Battle of Mohi. When Subutai heard in 1242 that Grand Khan Ög
2 CONC edei had died the previous year, the Mongol army retreated eastward, because Subutai had three princes of the blood in his command and Genghis Khan had made clear that all descendants of the Khagan (Grand Khan) should return to th
2 CONC e Mongol capital of Karakorum for the kurultai which would elect the next Khagan. The Polish people, unaware of the reason why the Mongols left so suddenly, simply assumed that they had been defeated in battle. [edit]Duchy of Aust
2 CONC ria for Přemyslids
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 June 1246, Frederick II of Austria was killed in a battle against Béla IV of Hungary. The battle took place by the river Leitha. He was childless and had never designated a heir. [18] The matter of his succession would resul
2 CONC t in years of disputes among various heirs. Wenceslaus' foreign policy became focused on acquiring Austria for the Přemyslid dynasty. Meanwhile Emperor Frederick II managed to once again place Austria under direct imperial rule. H
2 CONC owever imperial governor Otto von Eberstein had to content with an Austrian rebellion, preventing immediate benefits from the annexation of the Duchy. [19] The Privilegium Minus, the document which had elevated Austria to a Duchy 
2 CONC on 17 September 1156, allowed for the female line of the House of Babenberg to succeed to the throne. Gertrude, Duchess of Austria, niece of the late Frederick II, thus was able to claim the Duchy in her own right. Wenceslaus arra
2 CONC nged for her marriage to his eldest son, Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia. Vladislaus was declared a jure uxoris Duke of Austria and managed to secure the support of part of the Austrian nobility. On 3 January 1247, Vladislaus died
2 CONC  suddenly and the initial plan of Wenceslaus was negated. Gertrude continued her claim and proceeded to marry Herman VI, Margrave of Baden. [20] [21] [edit]The rebellion
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1248, Wenceslaus had to deal with a rebellion of the Bohemian nobility, led by his own son Ottokar II. Ottokar had been enticed by discontented nobles to lead the rebellion, during which he received the nickname "the younger Ki
2 CONC ng" (mladší král). Wenceslaus managed to defeat the rebels and imprisoned his son. [22]Ottokar II held the title of King of Bohemia from 31 July 1248 to November, 1249. [23] By the end of 1250, both the Emperor and Herman VI were 
2 CONC deceased. The latter having never been accepted by the Austrian nobles. Gertrude and their only son Frederick I, Margrave of Baden continued their claim. Wenceslaus led a successful invasion of Austria, completed by 1251. [24] Wen
2 CONC ceslaus released Ottokar II and named him margrave of Moravia. Wenceslaus had Ottokar proclaimed Duke of Austria and acclaimed by the nobility. In order to secure dynastic rights to Austria, Wenceslaus had another female Babenberg
2 CONC  proclaimed Duchess and betrothed to his son. Margaret, Duchess of Austria was a sister of Duke Frederick II and an aunt of Gertrude. She was also the widow of Henry (VII) of Germany who had died in 1242. However, Margaret was muc
2 CONC h older than Ottokar. Their marriage took place on 11 February 1252. [25] [26] Wenceslaus did not enjoy his victory for long. He died on 23 September 1253 and Ottokar II succeeded him. [27] [28]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenceslaus_I_of_Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus I Premyslid (Czech Václav I.) (c. 1205 – September 23, 1253) was King of Bohemia from 1230 to 1253.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Wenceslaus was a son of Ottokar I of Bohemia and his second wife Constance of Hungary. He was a younger, paternal half-brother to Margarethe of Bohemia and Božislava of Bohemia. His half-sisters were respectively the first 
2 CONC Queen consort of Valdemar II of Denmark and the first wife of Henry I, Count of Ortenburg. His sister Judith of Bohemia was married to Bernhard von Spanheim, Duke of Carinthia.His sister Anna of Bohemia was married to Henry II the
2 CONC  Pious, Duke of Wrocław. His sister Agnes of Bohemia was Mother Superior of the Franciscan Poor Clares nuns of Prague. In 1989, Agnes was canonized as a saint by Pope John Paul II.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His maternal grandparents were Béla III of Hungary and his second wife Agnes of Antioch (Agnes de Châtillon).[2] Agnes was a daughter of Raynald of Châtillon and Constance of Antioch (joint princes of Antioch).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children In 1224, Wenceslaus married Kunigunde of Hohenstaufen, second daughter of Philip of Swabia, King of Germany and his wife, Irene Angelina.[4] Her paternal grandparents were Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor and 
2 CONC Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy.[5] Her maternal grandparents were Isaac II Angelos, Byzantine Emperor and his first wife Herina.[6] Wenceslaus encouraged large numbers of Germans to settle in the villages and towns in Bohemia an
2 CONC d Moravia. Stone buildings began to replace wooden ones in Prague as a result of the influence of the new settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus and Kunigunde had five known children[7]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia (c. 1228 – 3 January 1247). Ottokar II of Bohemia (c. 1230 – 26 August 1278). Beatrix of Bohemia (c. 1231 – 27 May 1290). Married Otto III, Margrave of Brandenburg. Agnes of Bohemia (died 10 August 
2 CONC 1268). Married Henry III, Margrave of Meissen. An unnamed daughter. Died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early reign On 6 February 1228, Wenceslaus was crowned as co-ruler of the Kingdom of Bohemia with his father. On 15 December 1230, Ottokar died and Wenceslaus succeeded him as the senior King of Bohemia.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT His early reign was preoccupied by the threat to Bohemia posed by Frederick II, Duke of Austria. The expansionism of Frederick caused the concern and protestation of several other rulers. In 1236, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONC was involved in a war against the Lombard League. The Emperor demanded Wenceslaus and other rulers of the Holy Roman Empire to lend him part of their own troops for his war effort. Wenceslaus led a group of princes who expressed t
2 CONC heir reluctance to divert any troops from the defense of their own territories, citing fear of invasion from the Duchy of Austria. They requested imperial intervention in the situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June, 1236, the Emperor imposed an imperial ban on the Duke of Austria. Troops dispatched against the Duke, forced him to flee Vienna for Wiener Neustadt. He would continue to rule a rump state for the following year. The Emper
2 CONC or declared direct imperial rule in both Austria and the Duchy of Styria, also held by the fleeing Duke. Ekbert von Andechs-Meranien, former Bishop of Bamberg was installed as governor in the two Duchies.[8] Ekbert would govern fr
2 CONC om February to his death on 5 June 1237.[9] Wenceslaus was hardly pleased with this apparent expansion of direct imperial authority close to his borders. Wenceslaus and Duke Frederick formed an alliance against the Emperor. Freder
2 CONC ick the Emperor chose to lift the ban in 1237 rather than maintain another open front. Wenceslaus managed to negotiate the expansion of Bohemia north of the Danube, annexing territories offered by Duke Frederick in order of formin
2 CONC g and maintaining their alliance.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus and Frederick also found another ally in the person of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria. In June, 1239, Wenceslaus and Otto left the Reichstag at Eger, abandoning the service of excommunicated Emperor Frederick II. 
2 CONC Despite their intent to elect an antiking[7][10] no such election would take place until 1246. In 1246, Henry Raspe, Landgrave of Thuringia was elected King of Germany in opposition to Emperor Frederick II and Conrad IV of Germany
2 CONC .[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mongol invasion In 1241 Wenceslaus successfully repelled a raid on Bohemia by forces serving under Batu Khan and Subutai of the Mongol Empire as part of the Mongol invasion of Europe. The Mongols did not send their main army to th
2 CONC e Kingdom of Poland, Bohemia and Silesia and only Moravia suffered devastation at their hands. The raids into these four areas were led by Baidar, Kadan and Orda Khan with a force of around 20,000 Mongols. Following the Mongol vic
2 CONC tory at the Battle of Legnica, Wenceslaus fell back to gather reinforcements from Thuringia and Saxony, but was overtaken by the Mongol vanguard at Kłodzko. However, the Bohemian cavalry easily fended off the Mongol detachment. As
2 CONC  Baidar and Kadan's orders had been to serve as a diversion, they turned away from Bohemia and Poland and went southward to join Batu and Subutai, who had crushed the Hungarians at the Battle of Mohi.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Subutai heard in 1242 that Grand Khan Ögedei had died the previous year, the Mongol army retreated eastward, because Subutai had three princes of the blood in his command and Genghis Khan had made clear that all descendants o
2 CONC f the Khagan (Grand Khan) should return to the Mongol capital of Karakorum for the kurultai which would elect the next Khagan. The Polish people, unaware of the reason why the Mongols left so suddenly, simply assumed that they had
2 CONC  been defeated in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchy of Austria for Přemyslids On 15 June 1246, Frederick II of Austria was killed in a battle against Béla IV of Hungary. The battle took place by the river Leitha. He was childless and had never designated an heir.[8] The matte
2 CONC r of his succession would result in years of disputes among various heirs. Wenceslaus' foreign policy became focused on acquiring Austria for the Přemyslid dynasty. Meanwhile Emperor Frederick II managed to once again place Austri
2 CONC a under direct imperial rule. However imperial governor Otto von Eberstein had to content with an Austrian rebellion, preventing immediate benefits from the annexation of the Duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Privilegium Minus, the document which had elevated Austria to a Duchy on 17 September 1156, allowed for the female line of the House of Babenberg to succeed to the throne. Gertrude, Duchess of Austria, niece of the late Freder
2 CONC ick II, thus was able to claim the Duchy in her own right. Wenceslaus arranged for her marriage to his eldest son, Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia. Vladislaus was declared a jure uxoris Duke of Austria and managed to secure the su
2 CONC pport of part of the Austrian nobility. On 3 January 1247, Vladislaus died suddenly and the initial plan of Wenceslaus was negated. Gertrude continued her claim and proceeded to marry Herman VI, Margrave of Baden.[12][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT The rebellion In 1248, Wenceslaus had to deal with a rebellion of the Bohemian nobility, led by his own son Ottokar II. Ottokar had been enticed by discontented nobles to lead the rebellion, during which he received the nickname "
2 CONC the younger King" (mladší král). Wenceslaus managed to defeat the rebels and imprisoned his son.[14] Ottokar II held the title of King of Bohemia from 31 July 1248 to November, 1249.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT By the end of 1250, both the Emperor and Herman VI were deceased. The latter having never been accepted by the Austrian nobles. Gertrude and their only son Frederick I, Margrave of Baden continued their claim. Wenceslaus led a suc
2 CONC cessful invasion of Austria, completed by 1251.[7] Wenceslaus released Ottokar II and named him margrave of Moravia. Wenceslaus had Ottokar proclaimed Duke of Austria and acclaimed by the nobility. In order to secure dynastic righ
2 CONC ts to Austria, Wenceslaus had another female Babenberg proclaimed Duchess and betrothed to his son. Margaret, Duchess of Austria was a sister of Duke Frederick II and an aunt of Gertrude. She was also the widow of Henry (VII) of G
2 CONC ermany who had died in 1242. However, Margaret was much older than Ottokar. Their marriage took place on 11 February 1252.[15][16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus did not enjoy his victory for long. He died on 23 September 1253 and Ottokar II succeeded him.[7][15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ratings reign of Wenceslas I Under the reign of Wenceslas I of Bohemia comes to gothic lifestyle, culture and jousting, tournaments, spread popularity of courtly poetry and songs. His government is associated with the attachment o
2 CONC f Czech statehood, Czech increasing influence in Europe, the rise of Czech nobility, construction and urban development, trade and crafts. Václav, like his father and son, supported the arrival of ethnic Germans into the country. 
2 CONC He was also the first to provide privileges to the Jews who paid for it, however, considerable amounts. During his reign, the Czech state becomes more expansive and solid, coherent with the government of King Wenceslas I became a 
2 CONC feared Central Powers. Thanks therefore ranks among the top five kings of Czech history.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BOHEMIA.htm#Premysldied1239 Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT PŘEMYSL OTAKAR of Bohemia, son of WENZEL I King of Bohemia & his wife Kunigunde von Hohenstaufen (1233-killed in battle Dürnkrut 26 Aug 1278, bur Znaim Minoriten). The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "Wladislaum et 
2 CONC Przesemysl" as the two sons of "Rex Wencezslaus"[301]. Associate king of Bohemia 31 Jul 1248-Nov 1249. Markgraf of Moravia 1251. He succeeded as OTAKAR Duke of Austria and Steiermark in 1251. He succeeded his father in 1253 as OTA
2 CONC KAR PŘEMYSL II King of Bohemia. Crowned 1261. His possession of the duchies of Austria and Styria was legalised in 1262 by Richard of Cornwall King of Germany[302]. "Otakarus…rex Boemiæ, dux Austriæ et Stiriæ et marchio Moraviæ" f
2 CONC ounded Kloster Goldenkron by charter dated 1263[303]. "Otacharus…rex Boemorum dux Austrie et Styrie et marchio Moravie" confirmed the foundation of Kloster Hohenfurt by "Wockonis quondam marschalci regni nostri" by charter dated 1
2 CONC 7 Jul 1264[304]. King Richard appointed Otakar as protector of the royal domains east of the Rhine 7 Jan 1267[305]. He succeeded in 1269 as OTAKAR Duke of Carinthia, . In 1274, King Otakar ignored demands for the return to imperia
2 CONC l jurisdiction of the duchies of Austria, Carinthia and Styria. This proposal had been made in line with the policy of Rudolf I King of Germany, promulgated at the Diet of Nürnburg 19 Nov 1274, under which all properties unlawfull
2 CONC y appropriated since the deposition of Emperor Friedrich II in 1245 were to be returned to the empire. In accordance with the procedures established, the duchies were deemed forfeited after a year and a day[306]. King Rudolf decla
2 CONC red war on Bohemia, and King Otakar was obliged to abdicate under the temporary peace of 21 Nov 1276, which was confirmed by treaty 6 May 1277. Rudolf's position was confirmed definitively after he defeated King Otakar at the batt
2 CONC le of Marchfeld near Dürnkrut 26 Aug 1278. The Notæ Altahenses record that "Otakarus rex Boemie" was killed in battle "1278 VII Kal Sep"[307].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Hainburg 11 Feb 1252, dispensation 3o and 4o 5 Jul 1253, repudiated 1261) as her second husband, MARGARETA of Austria, widow of HEINRICH VII King of Germany, daughter of LEOPOLD VI Duke of Austria [Babenberg] & his wife
2 CONC  Theodora --- (-Burg Krumau am Kamp 2 Oct 1267, bur Lilienfeld). The Annales Mellicenses in 1226 record the marriage of "Margaretam filiam Liupoldi ducis Austrie" and "Heinricus filius imperatoris Friderici"[308]. The Notæ Sancti 
2 CONC Emeranni record the marriage in 1225 at Nürnberg of "Heinricus rex" and "Constantiam filiam Liupoldi ducis Austrie"[309]. She was crowned Queen of Germany 28 Mar 1227 at Aachen. She lived in a Dominican convent at Trier after the 
2 CONC death of her first husband[310]. The Continuatio Garstensis records the second marriage "apud Heimburch" of "Margaretam viduam regis Heinrici, filiam ducis Leupoldi" and "Otakarus marchio Moravie"[311]. The Canonicorum Pragensium 
2 CONC Continuationes Cosmæ records the marriage "in Henburk III Id Feb 1252" of "Prziemysl filius regis Wenceslai" and "Margaretham viduam filiam Leupoldi ducis Austriæ"[312]. The Chronicon Francisci records the marriage in 1252 of "Ott
2 CONC akarus Rex Boemiæ" and "Margaretham, quondam Romanorum Regina"[313]. "Margareta domina regni Boemie ducissa Austrie et Styrie marchionissa Moravie" granted "comitiam in Rakz" to "Wocconi de Rosenberch" by charter dated 1260[314]. 
2 CONC The Altahenses Annales record that "Otaker rex" repudiated his first wife "sine iudicio ecclesie"[315]. The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records that "regina Margareta" left Bohemia for Austria "XV Kal Nov 1261"[316
2 CONC ]. The necrology of Lilienfeld records the death "IV Kal Nov" of "Margareta quondam regina Romanorum filia ducis Leupoldi" and her burial next to her father "in Campo Liliorum"[317]. The necrology of Kloster Neuburg records the de
2 CONC ath "IV Kal Nov" of "Margareta filia ducis Liupoldi regina Romanorum ducissa Austrie et Stirie"[318].
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (Pressburg 25 Oct 1261) as her first husband, KUNIGUNDA [Kunguta] Rostislavna, daughter of ROSTISLAV Mikhailovich ex-Grand Prince of Kiev, ex-Prince of Galich, Ban of Mačva & his wife Anna of Hungary (1245-9 Sep 1285, b
2 CONC ur Prague Erlöserkirche). The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records the marriage "VIII Kal Nov 1261" of "Cunegundem filiam Hostislai ducis Bulgarorum" and the king "in castello Ungarias…Possen"[319]. The Chronica Pra
2 CONC gensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the second marriage of "Rex Przsemysl" and "Chungundam, Regis de Mazouia filiam"[320]. The Altahenses Annales record the marriage in 1261 of "Otaker rex" and "Chunigundem filiam Regis Ruscie, n
2 CONC eptem Bele regis Ungarie ex filia"[321]. She married secondly (Prague 2 Jun 1284) as his second wife, Zaviš von Rosenberg zu Skalitz und Falkenstein (-beheaded Schloß Frauenberg 24 Aug 1290, bur Hohenfurt). The Chronica Pragensis 
2 CONC (Chronicon Francisci) records the second marriage of "rex Wencezslaus…mater…eius" and "quondam Baronem…Zauissium" by whom she had a son "in Moravia…Iohannes" who joined "ordine Cruciferorum Christo"[322]. The Cronica Aulæ Regine r
2 CONC ecords the capture and death of “Zawissium...castrum...Vroburg”[323].
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): --- Palczierzik, daughter of ---. The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[324]. Pulkawa’s 
2 CONC Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...do
2 CONC mino de Strakonicz, secundam de Wartemberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[325]. Friess suggests that Pulkawa’s identification of Otakar’s mistress results from confusion with another source stating that the king’s dau
2 CONC ghter married “Heinrich V von Kuenring-Weitra” (see below)[326].
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): ---. The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Otakar Přemysl II & his second wife had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. HEINRICH (1262-1263). The Cronica Aulæ Regine records that “rex Ottacarus” and his second wife had “tres filios”, of whom “duobus mox in infantia defunctis”, while “iunior” succeeded their father[327]. The primary source which 
2 CONC confirms his name has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. son (-young). The Cronica Aulæ Regine records that “rex Ottacarus” and his second wife had “tres filios”, of whom “duobus mox in infantia defunctis”, while “iunior” succeeded their father[328].
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. KUNIGUNDE (Jan 1265-27 Nov 1321, bur Prague St Veit). The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the baptism of "Rex Przsemysl…filiæ suæ primogenitæ" in 1265[329]. The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records 
2 CONC that "filia Regis…Chungundis" became a nun "ad sanctum Franciscum" in 1276, but that she was later taken from the monastery by her brother and married to "Duci Mazouiæ", after whose death she became "Abbatissa ad sanctum Georgium 
2 CONC in castro Pragensis"[330]. The primary source which confirms her first betrothal has not yet been identified. The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the death in 1321 of "Regis Przemysl filia, Abbatissa ad sanctum Ge
2 CONC orgium in castro Pragensi…Chunegundis" and her burial in the monastery[331]. Betrothed ([1276]%29 to HARTMANN von Habsburg Graf von Kiburg, son of RUDOLF I Graf von Habsburg King of Germany & his first wife Gertrud [Anna] von Hohe
2 CONC nberg [Zollern] (Rheinfelden 1263-drowned between Breisach and Strasbourg 21 Dec 1281, bur Basel Münster). m (1291, divorced before 19 Dec 1302) as his second wife, BOLESŁAW II Prince of Plock, son of SIEMOWIT I Prince of Mazovia 
2 CONC [Piast] & his wife Pereiaslava Daniilovich of Galich (after 1251-20 Apr 1313). He succeeded his brother in 1294 as BOLESŁAW II Prince of Mazovia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. AGNES (5 Sep 1269-Prague 17 May 1296, bur Prague, Clarissan convent). The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records the birth "Non Sep 1269" of "regi Otakaro filia", but does not name her[332]. The Chronica Pragensis 
2 CONC (Chronicon Francisci) records the betrothal in 1278 of "Wencezslaus filius regis Boemiæ" and "filiam Domini Rudolphi Electi" and of "filius Electi filiam Regis Boemiæ"[333]. The Dytheri de Helmestat Notæ Historicæ record that "rex
2 CONC  Rudolfus…filium suum dominum Hartmudum [error for Rudolfum]" married "sororem regis Bohemie"[334]. After her husband died, she lived at Schloß Brugg im Aargau until 1295, thereafter becoming a nun at the Clarissan convent of Prag
2 CONC ue. The Chronicon Francisci records the death "XVI Kal Jun" in 1296 of "Domina Agnes soror Regis Boemiæ, Ducissa Austriæ, relicta Ducis Rudolphi Romanorum Regis filii" and her burial "in Ecclesia sanctæ Claræ ordinis fratrum minor
2 CONC em apud sanctum Franciscum in Praga"[335]. m (contract Iglau 1278, Mar 1289) RUDOLF II von Habsburg ex-Duke of Austria, son of RUDOLF I Graf von Habsburg King of Germany & his first wife Gertrud [Anna] von Hohenberg (1270-Prague 1
2 CONC 0 May 1290, bur Prague Burg, transferred 1293 to St Veit’s Cathedral).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. WENZEL (17 Sep 1271-21 Jun 1305, bur Prague Königsaal). The Chronicon Francisci records the birth "in die beatorum Martyrum Cozmæ et Damiani" in 1271 of "Wencezslaus…Rex Boemiæ"[336]. He succeeded in 1283 as WENZEL II King of B
2 CONC ohemia. Imperial Elector 1289. Crowned King of Bohemia 2 Jun 1297. King of Poland 1300.
2 CONT
2 CONT - see below.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Otakar Přemysl II had [seven] illegitimate children by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. NIKOLAUS ([1255]-25 Jul 1318). The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik", adding that he was later inve
2 CONC sted as "Dux Opauiæ"[337]. Pulkawa’s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “poste
2 CONC a tres filias” who married “unam...domino de Strakonicz, secundam de Wartemberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[338]. He was legitimated 6 Oct 1360, but excluded from succession to the throne of Bohemia 20 Oct 1260. He
2 CONC rr von Troppau 4 Jul 1269. Ritter 1273. He was installed in 1281 as NIKOLAUS I Duke of Troppau.
2 CONT
2 CONT - DUKES of TROPPAU.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. [AGNES] . The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[339]. Pulkawa’s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252
2 CONC ) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...domino de Strakonicz, secundam de War
2 CONC temberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[340]. m [BAVOR] [III] Herr von Strakonicz, son of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. daughter. The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[341]. Pulkawa’s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252
2 CONC ) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...domino de Strakonicz, secundam de War
2 CONC temberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[342]. m --- Herr von Wartenberg, son of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. daughter. The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[343]. Pulkawa’s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252
2 CONC ) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...domino de Strakonicz, secundam de War
2 CONC temberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[344]. m ([1276]%29 WOK Herr von Krawarz, son of --- (-[1329]).
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. [JESEK ([1265/70]-26 Aug 1296, bur Wysehrad). The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified. Priest at Wysehrad 1290.]
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. GISELA [Elisabeth?] . The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[345]. The Cronica Aulæ Regine names “d
2 CONC omina Gisela coniuge domini Wikardi de Polna...regis Ottacari filia”[346]. Friess records Balbin noting the marriage of Otakar’s daughter Elisabeth and “Wikard de Polna, einem böhmischen Magnaten”, suggesting that she was the same
2 CONC  person as the other Elisabeth shown below, Vikard being her second husband[347]. Balbin in fact records “Gisela Wikardi de Polna uxor”, without any indication of the source on which this information is based[348]. m VIKARD Herr v
2 CONC on Polna Burggraf of Brno, son of --- (-after 1303).
2 CONT
2 CONT 12. [daughter. The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified. m (after May 1277) --- Herr von Weitra, son of ---.]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Otakar Přemysl II had one illegitimate child by [Mistress (2)]:
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. ELISABETH . A letter of King Otakar II dated Mar 1277 records that “H[enricus] de Witra...filius suus” had married “nostram...filiam”[349]. “Heinrich von Kuenring, Marschall und Capitaneus von Oesterrecih...seiner Gattin Elisa
2 CONC bet” settled differences with the provost of Klosterneuburg by charter dated 25 Mar 1276[350]. “Henricus senior de Witra, Chunigundis uxor nostra, Henricus et Albero filii nostri et Elisabet nurus mea Henrici filii nostri legitima
2 CONC ” granted property to Otto von Dünstein by charter dated 29 Jan 1278[351]. m (before 25 Mar 1276) HEINRICH [V] von Kuenring-Weitra, son of HEINRICH [IV] von Kuenring-Weitra & his wife Kunigunde --- (-Troppau Jul 1281). Marshall of
2 CONC  Austria. https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BOHEMIA.htm
