1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_de_Thuringe
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie of Thuringia, born on March 20, 1224 near'Eisenach in Thuringia and died on May 29, 1275, was duchess of Brabant as the Duke's second wife Henry II. She is the ancestor of the house of Hesse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie of Thuringia is the second child of Louis IV (1200–1227), landgrave of Thuringia, and his wife Elizabeth of Hungary (1207–1231), daughter of the king Andre II. She was born in the territory of landgraviate of Thuringia, his
2 CONC  father's property, probably at wartburg Castle or at the neighboring castle of Creuzburg. Louis IV died when she was three: left to join the sixth crusade under the leadership of the emperor Frederick II, he died of a febrile ill
2 CONC ness army camp in the Puglia.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the early death of their father, Sophie and her older brother Hermann II (1222–1241) were just young children. Their uncle Henry the Raspon assumes regency over Thuringia and, according to some sources, intrigues to officially 
2 CONC succeed his late brother. The widow Elizabeth had to go with her children to Marburg where she dies on November 17, 1231 ; she was canonized by the Pope Gregory IX at Pentecost, the May 27, 1235. Hermann II never actually reigned 
2 CONC and died ten years later, at the age of 19. A « black legend » accuses his uncle of poisoning him, despite scientific evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the year 1240, Sophie marries him Henry II, duke of Brabant. Henri's first wife, Marie de Hohenstaufen had died in 1235. Two children were born from her second marriage to Sophie : Henry Ier (1244–1308) says « the Child of 
2 CONC Brabant», the future landgrave of Hesse, and Élisabeth (1243–1261), married in 1254 to the duke Albert Ier from Brunswick-Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around the year 1245, Sophie had the Sainte-Elisabeth church built in Liège, in honor of his mother[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT In her later years, Sophie devoted herself to fighting for the heritage of the landgraves of Thuringia. After the death of his brother Hermann II, Emperor Frederick II promised to cede the fief to the margrave Henry III from Misni
2 CONC e, from the wettin's house, a nephew of Landgrave Louis IV and Sophie's cousin. He kept his commitment when Henry the Raspon died without issue in 1247, despite the protests of Sophie who considered her young son Henri Ier as legi
2 CONC timate heir and ensured the support of an assembly of nobles to Gudensberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, the conflict there thuringian War of Succession breaks out between Sophie the Margrave Henri III the Illustrious. The April 23, 1248, Sophie enters into a strategic alliance with the'Teutonic Order in Marburg, while the coun
2 CONC ts in Thuringia, including the houses of Schwarzburg, of Weimar-Orlamünde and of Stolberg, submitted to the Margrave of Misnie. Taking advantage of the absence of power imperial during the Great Interregnum, Henry III seizes almos
2 CONC t all of Thuringia up to Wartburg Castle. The March 2, 1250, Sophie also had to abandon guardianship of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, upon the marriage of her daughter Élisabeth, Sophie obtained help from her stepson Albert Ier from Brunswick, whose own sister Hélène (1231-1273) was engaged to Hermann II from Thuringia, as well as from his brother-in-la
2 CONC w Henry II from Anhalt-Aschersleben. In spring 1259, the allies once again attacked the regions of'Eisenach, Wartburg and Creuzburg. The conflict between Sophie and Henri III de Misnie lasts until 1263, when Albert Ier from Brunsw
2 CONC ick is completely beaten and captured by the Margrave's sons, Albert II and Thierry de Landsberg. The following year, Sophie had to give up Thuringia for good but managed to secure it landgraviate of Hesse to his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Ier, having married Adelaide (morte in 1263 in 1274), a second sister of Albert Ier from Brunswick, takes the title of « landgrave from Hesse ». From 1273, he supported the new king Rudolph Ier from Habsburg ; in return, he 
2 CONC was elevated to the rank of prince of the Holy Roman Empire in 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through their son, Sophie is the founder of the Brabant dynasty of Hesse. She died in 1275 and was buried in the'abbey church from the'cistercian abbey from Villers, in Brabant.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portail:Brabant_flamand
