2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Gorizia-Tyrol From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth de Gorizia de Tirol de Carantania (c. 1262 – 28 October 1312) was queen consort of the Holy Roman Empire and Duchess of Austria. [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Meinhard II, Duke of Carinthia, count of Gorizia and Tyrol. Her mother was Elisabeth of Bavaria. She was a daughter of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria and his wife Agnes, herself daughter of Hen
2 CONC ry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Agnes of Hohenstaufen. Her mother was also the widow of Conrad IV of Germany. Therefore, the young Elisabeth was a half-sister of Conradin, King of Jerusalem and Duke of Swabia. The younger El
2 CONC isabeth was from 1274 the wife of the future Albert I of Germany, one of the founders of the House of Habsburg, thus becoming daughter-in-law of the King of the Romans. Her husband, then a Count of Habsburg was invested as Duke of
2 CONC  Austria and Styria in December 1282 by his father the King Rudolf. They solidified their rule in what was to become the Habsburg patrimony, also by help of Elisabeth's father who in his turn in 1286 was created Duke of Carinthia.
2 CONC  In 1298, her husband was finally elected king upon the end of the reign of Adolf of Nassau. Her husband was murdered on 1 May 1308 by his nephew John "the Parricide" in Windisch, located in modern-day Switzerland. [edit]Marriage 
2 CONC and children
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married in Vienna on 20 December 1274 to the future Albert I of Germany. Their children were: Rudolph III (ca. 1282–4 July 1307, Horazdiowitz), Married but line extinct and predeceased his father. Frederick I (1289–13 Janu
2 CONC ary 1330, Gutenstein). Married but line extinct. Leopold I (4 August 1290–28 February 1326, Strassburg). Married but line extinct. Albert II (12 December 1298, Vienna–20 July 1358, Vienna). Heinrich (1299–3 February 1327, Bruck an
2 CONC  der Mur). Married but line extinct. Meinhard, 1300 died young. Otto (23 July 1301, Vienna–26 February 1339, Vienna). Married but line extinct. Anna 1280?, Vienna–19 March 1327, Breslau), married: in Graz ca. 1295 to Margrave Herm
2 CONC ann of Brandenburg; in Breslau 1310 to Duke Heinrich VI of Breslau. Agnes (18 May 1281–10 June 1364, Königsfelden), married in Vienna 13 February 1296 King Andrew III of Hungary. Elisabeth (d. 19 May 1353), married 1304 Frederick 
2 CONC IV, Duke of Lorraine. Katharina (1295–18 January 1323, Naples), married 1316 Charles, Duke of Calabria. Jutta (d. 1329), married in Baden 26 March 1319 Count Ludwig VI of Öttingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/42763973/elisabeth-von_görz
2 CONT
2 CONT German royalty. Born the second daughter of Meinhard II/IV, Duke of Carinthia, count of Görz and Tyrol. She married Albrecht I around 1276 in Vienna and was crowned roman-german queen in 1299 in Nürnberg. She bore him twenty-one c
2 CONC hildren, of which nine died immediately after the birth and remained nameless. She founded the Abbey of Königsfelden at the place where her husband was murdered. Together with him and other Habsburger bodies she was transferred to
2 CONC  Sankt Paul in 1809
