0 @N6000000002187834722@ NOTE Meinhard I of Gorizia-Tyrol
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Meinhard I (born c. 1200 or 1205, died in January/February of 1258), was Count of Gorizia and Tyrol.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also father of Meinhard II of Tyrol and Albert II of Gorizia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He came in control over his family's Gorizian possessions in 1232, but it his father-in-law Albert III of Tyrol as a fief. Between 1248 and 1250, he operated as the imperial governor of Styria and Carniola, from 1250 onwards also 
1 CONC in Austria. In 1252, he was forced to take certain possessions in Lienz as fiefs from the Philipp, Archbishop of Salzburg, in the Treaty of Lieserhofen and to give his sons to him as hostages. After the death of Albert III, Meinha
1 CONC rd, and his brother-in-law, Gebhard of Hirschberg, split Tyrol, of which Meinhard took the southern part.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Albert III Count of Tyrol Succeeded by:
1 CONT Meinhard II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT ??? Count of Gorizia 
0 @N6000000000269788072@ NOTE RUDOLF von Baden, son of HERMANN V Markgraf von Baden & his wife Irmgard von Braunschweig ([1230]-19 Nov 1288, bur Lichtenthal).  He succeeded in 1249 as RUDOLF I Markgraf von Baden.  He was one of the few Swabian noblemen who rem
1 CONC ained loyal to Konrad IV King of Germany against the papal party and the anti-king Willem II Count of Holland in 1251[21].  in Pforzheim 1257.  The necrology of Salzburg St Rudpert records the death "XIII Kal Dec" of "Rudolfus mar
1 CONC chio de Baden fundator mon Lucidenallis"[22].
1 CONT
1 CONT m (before 20 May 1257) KUNIGUNDE von Eberstein, daughter of OTTO I von Eberstein ([1230]-Lichtenthal 21 Apr 1290, bur Lichtenthal).  Heiress of ½ Eberstein.  The Annales Sindelfingenses record the death "1284 11 Kal Mai in castro 
1 CONC Libincelle" of "comitissa de Baden"[23].
1 CONT
