0 @N6000000004533643892@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Frederick II of Hohenstaufen (1090 – 1147) was duke of Swabia,succeeding his father, duke Frederick I in 1105. In 1121 marriedJudith of Bavaria, a member of the powerful House of Guelph.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Emperor Henry V, his uncle, Frederick stood forelection as King of Germany with the support his younger brotherConrad of Swabia and several houses. However, he lost this election of1125 to Lothar II, crowned Empero
1 CONC r later in 1133.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick's descendents were:
1 CONT
1 CONT From Judith of Bavaria (1100-1132)
1 CONT Frederick III Barbarrossa (1122-1190), duke of Swabia and Holy RomanEmperor as Frederick I
1 CONT Bertha of Hohenstaufen (1123-1195), married Matthias I, Duke ofLorraine
1 CONT From Agnes of Saarbrücken (d.~1147)
1 CONT Conrad of Hohenstaufen, Palatine Count of the Rhein (1134-1195)
1 CONT Judith of Hohenstaufen (1135-1191), married Ludwig II, Duke ofThuringia
0 @N6000000005599009152@ NOTE Frederick II of Hohenstaufen (1090 - 1147) was duke of Swabia, succeed ing his father, duke Frederick I in 1105. In 1121 he married Judith o f Bavaria, a member of the powerful House of Guelph.  On the death ofE mperor Henry V, hi
1 CONC s uncle, Frederick stood for election as King of Ger many with the support of his younger brother Conrad of Swabia and seve ral houses. However, he lost this election of 1125 to Lothar II, crown ed Emperor later in 1133.
1 CONT
1 CONT A conflict erupted between Frederick and his supporters, and Lothar. E ncouraged by Albert, Archbishop of Mainz, who loathed the supporters o f the late Emperor Henry V, Lothar besieged Nuremberg in 1127. Frederi ck's second wife,
1 CONC  Agnes, was the niece of his old enemy Albert of Main z. According to Otto of Freising, Frederick was "so faithful a knightt o his sovereign and so helpful a friend to his uncle that by valor he supported the tottering honor of th
1 CONC e realm, fighting manfully against its foes..." Frederick's descendants were:
1 CONT
1 CONT With Judith of Bavaria (1100-1132): Frederick III Barbarossa (1122-119 0), duke of Swabia and Holy Roman Emperor as Frederick I and Judith o f Hohenstaufen (1123-1195), married Matthias I, Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT With Agnes of Saarbrücken (d.~1147): Conrad of Hohenstaufen, Count Pal atine of the Rhine (1134-1195)
1 CONT Clarissa of Hohenstaufen (1135-1191), married Ludwig II, Duke of Thuri ngia
0 @N6000000002187690013@ NOTE Frederick II, Duke of Swabia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick II (1090 – 6 April 1147), called the One-Eyed, "of Hohenstauffen" was duke of Swabia. He was the eldest son of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia, and Agnes of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 1105. In 1121 he married Judith of Bavaria, a member of the powerful House of Guelph.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Emperor Henry V, his uncle, Frederick stood for election as King of the Romans with the support of his younger brother Conrad, duke of Franconia and several houses. However, he lost this election of 1125 to Lothar 
1 CONC II, crowned Emperor later in 1133.
1 CONT
1 CONT A conflict erupted between Frederick and his supporters, and Lothar. Encouraged by Albert, Archbishop of Mainz, who loathed the supporters of the late Emperor Henry V, Lothar besieged Nuremberg in 1127. Frederick relieved the sieg
1 CONC e of Nuremberg in 1127 and occupied Speyer in 1128. The attempt of Henry the Proud, duke of Bavaria, to capture Frederick during negotiations failed (1129). However, afterwards supporters of Lothar won a number of victories both i
1 CONC n Germany and in Italy. Speyer (1129), Nuremberg (1130) and Ulm (1134) were captured and in October 1134 Frederick submitted to the emperor. In 1135 both Frederick and Conrad were finally reconciled with Lothar. After Lothar's dea
1 CONC th (1137) and election of Conrad as King of the Romans (1138) Frederick supported his brother in the struggle with Guelphs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick's second wife, Agnes, was the niece of his old enemy Albert of Mainz.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Otto of Freising, Frederick was "so faithful a knight to his sovereign and so helpful a friend to his uncle that by valor he supported the tottering honor of the realm, fighting manfully against its foes..."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Frederick's descendants were:
1 CONT
1 CONT from Judith of Bavaria (d. 1130 or 1131), daughter of Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria 
1 CONT Frederick III Barbarossa (1122-1190), duke of Swabia and Holy Roman Emperor as Frederick I 
1 CONT Berthe of Hohenstaufen (1123-1195), married Matthias I, Duke of Lorraine 
1 CONT from Agnes of Saarbrücken (d.~1147) 
1 CONT Conrad of Hohenstaufen (also called Konrad) (1134/1136-1195), Count Palatine of the Rhine 
1 CONT Judith of Hohenstaufen (1135-1191), married Louis II, Landgrave of Thuringia 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Otto of Freising 
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 45-25, 166-25 
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Swabia family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Genealogy 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Swabia
1 CONT 1105–1147 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick III Barbarossa 
0 @N6000000005599009117@ NOTE Conrad of Hohenstaufen (born probably 1134/1136; died 8 November 1195 ) was the first hereditary Count Palatine of the Rhine.  His parents w ere Frederick II of Swabia (1090-1147), Duke of Swabia, and his secon d wife Agnes of Saa
1 CONC rbrucken.
1 CONT
1 CONT Young Conrad, the only half-brother of Frederick Barbarossa, receivedt he family's possessions around Franconia and Rhineland, particularlyth ose of his mother's ancestry.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1156 on the occasion of the Reichstag at Worms, Emperor Frederick B arbarossa conferred upon his half-brother the dignity of Pfalzgraf (Co unt Palatine, of the Rhine), as well as the Vogtei of Schönau Abbey an d of the chapter 
1 CONC of Worms Cathedral, besides the Staufen family estate s in the regions of Speyer and Worms. From about 1160 Conrad was marri ed to Irmingard of Henneberg (d. 1197) as his second wife, daughter o f Count Bertold I of Henneberg, Bur
1 CONC ggraf of Würzburg. This brought hi m the possession of the Vogtei of Lorsch Abbey. His endeavours to exte nd his area of influence brought him into conflict with the Bishops o f Trier and Cologne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad and both his wives were buried in Schönau Abbey near Heidelberg . His two sons did not survive to continue the family. His inheritanc e passed to his daughter Agnes and her husband Henry V, of the House o f Brunswick. (Thei
1 CONC r heiress would, in turn, pass the inheritance to th e Wittelsbach dynasty who thereby became the well-known lords of the P alatinate and Electors Palatine.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad married firstly a daughter, whose name is not known, of Count G ottfried I of Sponheim, who probably died in 1159 or 1160 and was buri ed in Schönau Abbey. By her he had a son, Gottfried of Staufen (died p robably in 1187 o
1 CONC r 1188).
1 CONT
1 CONT He married secondly Irmingard of Henneberg, by whom he had three child ren: Friedrich (died 3 September before 1189); Konrad (died probably i n 1186), buried in Schönau Abbey; Agnes (died 9 May 1204), buried in S t. Marien in Stad
1 CONC e; married late 1193 at Burg Stahleck Henry V, Duke o f Brunswick (died 28 April 1227), Count Palatine of the Rhine from 119 5 to 1212.
