0 @N6000000002187828754@ NOTE Henry I the Bearded
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I the Bearded (Polish: Henryk I Brodaty; German: Heinrich I der Bärtige,; 1163 - March 19, 1238), of the Silesian line of the Piast dynasty, was Duke of Lower Silesia from 1201. He was later also Duke of Lesser Poland and th
1 CONC us senior prince of all Poland from 1232 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Glogów, Silesia, in southwestern Poland, he married Hedwig of Andechs (Polish: Jadwiga). His son and successor, Duke Henry II the Pious, would later be killed battling the Mongols at Legnica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I had succeeded his father, Duke Boleslaw I the Tall, who had received the ducal position following an 1163 intervention, in a succession dispute, by Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I strove to reunite the provinces of fragmented Poland, and succeeded in bringing under his authority Silesia, Lesser Poland and part of Greater Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Poland (966-1385) 
1 CONT Dukes of Silesia 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This biography of a Polish noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT  This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Boleslaw the Tall Duke of Wroclaw
1 CONT 1232-1238 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry II the Pious 
0 @N6000000002187828211@ NOTE Henry II the Pious
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II the Pious (Polish: Henryk II Pobozny; German Heinrich II der Fromme; 1196/1207 - April 9, 1241) of Silesia was the third son of Henryk I the Bearded and of Hedwig of Andechs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry the Pious succeeded his father in 1238 and became the most powerful of all the Polish dukes. He united the majority of Polish territories (Silesia, Greater Poland and Little Poland).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1239, Henry the Pious supported the Pope in the latter's conflict with Emperor Frederick II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry the Pious, the father of Henryk III the White, was able to defend his territories against Brandenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed fighting the Mongols at the Battle of Legnica (1241).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Poland (966-1385) 
1 CONT Piast dynasty 
1 CONT Dukes of Silesia 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT  This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT This biography of a Polish noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry I the Bearded Duke of Wroclaw
1 CONT 1238-1241 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Boleslaw II the Bald 
0 @N6000000002187845585@ NOTE Adelaide, Countess Palatine of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide (or Alice, Alix; died 8 March 1279, Evian) was countess palatine of Burgundy from 1248 until her death. She was also wife of the brother and heir of the count of Savoy from 1267 until her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide was the daughter of Otto IV (Otto II of Burgundy), and Beatrice II, Countess Palatine of Burgundy. She inherited the County after the death of her brother, Otto III, Count Palatine of Burgundy, in 1248. As countess, she c
1 CONC ame into conflict with King Rudolph I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide died in 1279 and was buried in the Abbey of Cherlieu near Besançon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide married Hugh, Count of Salins (died 1266), from a younger branch of male-line dynasty of Invrean-originated Counts of Burgundy and thus her mother's kinsman, around 1239. They had, among others, the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     1. Otto IV, Count of Burgundy (died 1302), married
1 CONT
1 CONT         in 1271 to 1. Philippa of Bar
1 CONT         in 1285 to 2. Mahaut of Artois, granddaughter of Louis IX of France
1 CONT
1 CONT     2. Renaud, Count of Montbéliard (died 1322)
1 CONT     3. John
1 CONT     4. Guia of Burgundy (died 1316), married in 1274 to Thomas III of Savoy, count in Piedmont and pretender of County of Savoy and son of Thomas II of Savoy, thus a nephew of her second husband.
1 CONT     5. Hugh (died 1312)
1 CONT     6. Police (Hippolyta), married to Aymer IV, Count of Valence (1277-1330)
1 CONT     7. Elizabeth (d. 1275), married in 1250 to Hartman the Young, Count of Kyburg (d. 1263) and has a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT         Anne, married in 1273 to Eberhard of Habsburg-Laufeuburg
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 June 1267, Adelaide married Philip, defrocked archbishop of Lyon and the future Count of Savoy (died 1285). The marriage remained childless.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Otto III Countess Palatine of Burgundy
1 CONT 1248–1279 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto IV
