0 @N6000000002187746838@ NOTE Godfrey II of Leuven
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Godfrey II (c.1110 – 13 June 1142) was the count of Leuven (or Louvain) and Brussels, landgrave of Brabant, and margrave of Antwerp by inheritance from 23 January 1139. He was the son of Godfrey I and Ida of Chiny. He was also the
1 CONC  duke of Lower Lorraine (as Godfrey VII) by appointment in 1139 after the death of Duke Waleran.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was first associated with his father in 1136, when he first carried the ducal title. This was confirmed by Conrad III of Germany, who had married the sister of Godfrey's wife. Waleran left a son, Henry II of Limburg, who assert
1 CONC ed his father's ducal rights. Godfrey and Henry entered into a war in which the latter was decisively and quickly destroyed. Godfrey did not long enjoy his victory. He was killed by a disease of the liver two years thence. He was 
1 CONC buried in the church of Saint-Pierre in Leuven.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Lutgard, daughter of Berengar II of Sulzbach and sister of Gertrude, wife of Conrad III of Germany, and Bertha, wife of Manuel I Comnenus, the emperor of Byzantium. He was succeeded by his son Godfrey III in both the co
1 CONC unties and the duchy.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Waleran Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT 1139 – 1142 Succeeded by
1 CONT Godfrey VIII
0 @N6000000002187796972@ NOTE Godfrey IV, Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Godfrey IV (died 27 or 26 February 1076), known as the Hunchback, was a son of Godfrey the Bearded, whom he succeeded as duke of Lower Lorraine in 1069. His mother was Doda.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year of his succession, he married Matilda la Gran Contessa, daughter of Boniface III of Tuscany, and Beatrice of Bar. By this marriage, he succeeded his father as margrave of Tuscany, for his father had been married to Bea
1 CONC trice of Bar, the mother of Matilda. From 1071, Godfrey lived separate from his wife. The two spouses were on opposite sides in the Investiture Controversy: Matilda was a partisan of Pope Gregory VII and Godfrey of the Emperor Hen
1 CONC ry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He warred on Henry's behalf against Magnus, Duke of Saxony, in 1075 and on that of the bishop of Utrecht in 1076 against Dirk V of Holland and Robert I of Flanders. He was assassinated on the Scheldt on the eve of battle. His only
1 CONC  child was a daughter, Beatrice, by Matilda. He nominated his nephew Godfrey of Bouillon to succeed him, but the emperor appointed his own son, Conrad. Godfrey of Bouillon succeeded eventually in 1087 and gained fame on the First 
1 CONC Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT (broken link)*The assassination of Godfrey the Hunchback
1 CONT  This biography of a European noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Godfrey III Duke of Lower Lotharingia
1 CONT 1069 – 1076 Succeeded by
1 CONT Conrad
1 CONT Margrave of Tuscany
1 CONT 1069 – 1076 Succeeded by
1 CONT Matilda
