1 CONC  t end all his plans were." He had come to have his own vassals marrie d to his cousins, Baldwin's sister Princess Sibylla and half-sisterPri ncess Isabella. Sibylla's husband William of Montferrat had just died , leaving her preg
1 CONC nant with the future Baldwin V; William of Tyre, wh o was the chief negotiator in this dispute, told the count it would b e improper to marry her off again so soon. According to the chronicleo f Ernoul, Philip was also rebuffed by
1 CONC  Raymond III of Tripoli, who als o claimed the regency, as well as Raymond's supporters from the Ibelin s, who hoped to marry the princesses into their own family. Baldwin o f Ibelin insulted the count in public, and Philip left J
1 CONC erusalem in Oc tober to campaign in the north for the Principality of Antioch, partic ipating in an unsuccessful siege of Harim before returning home. Meanw hile, the Byzantine alliance against Egypt was abanonded, but in Novem be
1 CONC r, Baldwin IV and Raynald defeated Saladin at the Battle of Montgisa rd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip returned from Palestine in 1179, and Louis VII, now sick, name d him guardian of his young son Philip II. The count had the king marr ied to his niece, Isabelle of Hainaut, with an imprudently disproporti onate dowry: the C
1 CONC ounty of Artois. When Louis VII died, Philip II bega n to assert his independence. War broke out in 1180 and Picardy and th e Ile-de-France were devastated; King Philip refused to give open batt le and gained the upper hand, and B
1 CONC aldwin of Hainaut, at first alliedw ith his brother-in-law Count Philip, soon intervened on behalf of his son-in-law the King, in support of his daughter's interests. The dispu te between Count Philip and Baldwin was encouraged by
1 CONC  King Philip, wh o went so far as to name Baldwin his representation in negotiations wi th the Count.
1 CONT
1 CONT Count Philip's wife Elisabeth died childless in 1183, and King Philip seized Vermandois on behalf of Elisabeth's sister, Eleonore. Philip th en married Teresa of Portugal, daughter of Afonso I of Portugal, firs t king of that coun
1 CONC try, and Maud of Savoy. Teresa arrived with a larg e dowry, but this marriage was also childless.  Initially, he took up arms to defend what he believed were his rights to the area of Vermand ois and was supported by the Count of 
1 CONC Hainault.  Philippe II of Franc e quickly detached the Count of Hainault from Philip of Alsace's force s by threatening to repudiate Philip of Alsaces' daughter.  No actual battle occured and Philip retained the seisen of the coun
1 CONC ties of Saint -Quentin and Peronne along with the town of Ham.  Fearing that he woul d be surrounded by the royal domain of France and the County of Hainau t, Philip signed a peace treaty with his enemies on March 10, 1186, re cog
1 CONC nizing the cession of Vermandois to the king, although he was allow ed to retain the title Count of Vermandois for the remainder of his li fe.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1190 Philip took the cross for a second time and joined the Flemis h contingents which had already gone to Palestine. After arriving at t he Siege of Acre, he was stricken by the epidemic passing through thec rusader camp, and 
1 CONC died on August 1, 1191. His body was brought back t o Flanders by his wife, who acted as regent during his absence, and Ph ilip was buried in Clairvaux Abbey. He was succeeded by his sister Mar garet and his brother-in-law Baldwin
1 CONC  of Hainaut.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip seems to represent the end of one kind of feudal world, and th e beginning of a new type of sovereignty, put into practise by King Ph ilip: for the first time, a king of France ruled over a count of Fland ers. However, desp
1 CONC ite a costly war, the economic expansion of Flander s did not stop, as witnessed by the number of communal charters signe d by Count Philip, and by the end of his reign the county had enteredi nto a period of unprecedented prosper
1 CONC ity.  Philip also acted as patro n for Chrétien de Troyes while he was writing his last romance, Percev al, the Story of the Grail. In the opening lines, Chrétien praises Phi lip for providing him with the book he adapted into the
1 CONC  "best tale eve r told in a royal court". The work is unfinished, and was obviously be gun sometime before Philip's death.
