1 CONC ace in 1152 and the peaceful succession of one of Co nrad's family was secured.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad married twice: c. 1115, Gertrud von Komburg (died c. 1130-31) 1 136, Gertrud von Sulzbach (died 14 April 1146), whose sister Bertha ma rried Manuel I Comnenos.  He was never crowned emperor and continued t o style himself K
1 CONC ing of the Romans until his death. On his deathbed, i n the presence of only two witnesses, his nephew Frederick Barbarossa and the bishop of Bamberg, he allegedly designated Frederick Barbaross a his successor, rather than his ow
1 CONC n six-year-old son Frederick, who i nstead succeeded Barbarossa as Duke of Swabia. Frederick Barbarossa, w ho had accompanied his uncle on the unfortunate crusade, forcefully pu rsued his advantage and was duly elected king in Col
1 CONC ogne a few weeks l ater.
0 @N6000000002240801320@ NOTE When Emperor Henry V died childless in 1125, the electors chose Lothair, duke
1 CONT of Saxony to succeed Henry V. Conrad and and brother Frederick revolted
1 CONT because the hereditary principle of succession was rejected by the electors.
1 CONT On December 18, 1127, Conrad was elected antiking at Nurnberg and in 1128 was
1 CONT crowned king of Italy at Monza. Returning to Germany in 1132, Conrad fought
1 CONT Lothair until 1135, when he submitted, was pardoned and recovered his
1 CONT estates. After Lothair died in 1137, Conrad was elected German King.
