0 @N6000000002187645455@ NOTE Otto III, Duke of Swabia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto III (died 28 September 1057), called the White and known as Otto of Schweinfurt, was the margrave of the Nordgau (1024 – 1031) and duke of Swabia (1048 – 1057). He was the son of Henry of Schweinfurt, margrave of the Nordgau,
1 CONC  and Gerberga of Henneberg. He was one of the most powerful East Franconian princes by inheritance: having extensive land in the Radenzgau and Schweinfurt. In 1014, he first appears as count of Lower Altmühl (or Kelsgau) and, in 1
1 CONC 024, he inherits his father's march. In 1034, he became count of the Lower Naab. From then on to his appointment to Swabia, he takes part in many imperial expeditions into Bohemia, Hungary, and Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Ulm in January 1048, the Emperor Henry III appointed him duke of Swabia after a brief vacancy following the death of Otto II. He was loyal to Henry. He was engaged to marry Matilda, daughter of Boleslaus I of Poland, in 1035, b
1 CONC ut this was put off in favour of a marriage to Ermengard, a daughter of Ulric Manfred, Margrave of Turin, as part of Henry's Italian plans. He was otherwise inactive and died after nine years rule and was buried in Schweinfurt.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT By his marriage to Ermengard (died 29 April 1078), he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bertha or Alberada (died 1 April 1103), married firstly Herman II, Count of Kastl, and married secondly Frederick, Count of Kastl 
1 CONT Gisela, inherited Kulmbach and Plassenburg, married Berthold III, Count of Andechs 
1 CONT Judith (d.1104), married firstly Conrad I, Duke of Bavaria, and secondly Botho, Count of Pottenstein 
1 CONT Eilika, abbess of Niedermünster 
1 CONT Beatrice (1040 – 1140), inherited Schweinfut, married Henry II, Count of Hildrizhausen and Margrave of the Nordgau 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Otto II Duke of Swabia
1 CONT 1048–1057 Succeeded by
1 CONT Rudolf 
