0 @N6000000002187828274@ NOTE Boleslaw II the Bald
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaw II the Bald the Tollgate (Polish: Boleslaw II Lysy Rogatka) (1220/5 - December 26, 1278) was the eldest son of Henryk II the Pious.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's death, he became Duke of central Greater Poland (1241 - 1247) and Duke of Silesia (1241 -1248). After the partiton of Silesia, he was Duke of Legnica - Glogów. He lost Lubusz and Glogów in 1249. During his rule 
1 CONC there was a second mongol raid against Poland, led by Nogai Khan. He was Duke of Sroda Slaska from 1277.
1 CONT This biography of a Polish noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henryk II the Pious Duke of Wroclaw Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henryk III the White
1 CONT Wladyslaw of Salzburg
0 @N6000000002187828284@ NOTE Henryk III the White
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henryk III the White (Polish: Henryk III Bialy; 1227/30 - December 3, 1266) was Duke of Wroclaw from 1248.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Henry II the Pious and Anna, and father of Henryk IV Probus. He became Duke of Wroclaw after the partition of Lower Silesia in 1248.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Boleslaw II the Bald Duke of Wroclaw
1 CONT with Wladyslaw of Salzburg Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henryk IV Probus 
0 @N6000000002187828300@ NOTE Wladyslaw of Salzburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw of Salzburg was brother of Henryk III the White.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw was archbishop of Salzburg and an advocate, with Henryk, for the canonization of their grandmother, Jadwiga, Duchess of Silesia. She was canonized in 1267 as Saint Hedwig of Andechs.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT "Henryk III Bialy" ("Henryk III the White"), Encyklopedia Polski (Encyclopedia of Poland), Kraków, Wydawnictwo Ryszard Kluszczynski, 1996, p. 216.
0 @N6000000002188005305@ NOTE Frederick II, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick II, known as the Quarrelsome or the Warlike (German: Friedrich der Streitbare; 25 April 1211 – 15 June 1246), from the dynasty of the Babenbergers, was the duke of Austria and Styria from 1230 to 1246.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the third, but the second surviving son of Duke Leopold VI and Theodora Angelina, a Byzantine princess. The death of his older brother Henry in 1228 make him the new heir of the Duchy of Austria. Two years later (1230), his
1 CONC  father died and Frederick succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT His first spouse was another Byzantine princess named Sophia Laskarina, of the Laskaris dynasty, and his second wife was Agnes, the daughter of Otto I, Duke of Merania and Beatrice II, Countess of Burgundy. He had no surviving chi
1 CONC ldren, and the male line of the Babenberg dynasty ended with him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick was known as the Quarrelsome because of his frequent wars against his neighbors, primarily with Hungary, Bavaria and Bohemia. Even the Kuenringer family, which had so far been faithful to the ruling house, started an ins
1 CONC urgency as soon as his reign began. But most dangerous were his disputes with Emperor Frederick II, who ostracized him in 1236. During the years of his ban, Vienna became an imperial free city for some years. However, he managed t
1 CONC o maintain his position in Wiener Neustadt. In 1239, in a spectacular change in imperial politics, Frederick became one of the emperor's most important allies. Negotiations about the elevation of Vienna to a bishopric and of Austr
1 CONC ia (including Styria) to a kingdom were initiated. However, the duke's niece Gertrude would have had to marry the almost fifty-year-old emperor, which the girl refused.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year before his death, he finally succeeded in gaining the Duchy of Carniola, but his death led to it going to the duke of Carinthia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Frederick finally died in a battle against the Hungarian king Béla IV by the Leitha river.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the last Babenberg duke, Frederick the Quarrelsome signifies the end of an era in the history of Austria. With his overambitious plans, which were frequently foiled by his erratic character, he somewhat resembled his later succ
1 CONC essor Rudolf IV. As the Privilegium Minus also allowed women to inherit, his sister Margaret and his niece Gertrude would have been entitled to the throne. Gertrude first married Vladislav, Margrave of Moravia, who soon died, then
1 CONC  Herman VI, Margrave of Baden, who did not manage to maintain his position in Austria, and later Roman of Halicz, a relative of the king of Hungary. Margaret was married to Premysl Ottokar II of Bohemia, more than twenty years her
1 CONC  junior. Subsequently, Austria became of field of conflict between the Premyslid and Arpad dynasties, in which Ottokar would prevail until being overthrown by Rudolph of Habsburg.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold VI Duke of Austria
1 CONT Duke of Styria
1 CONT 1230-1246 Succeeded by
1 CONT Interregnum (to 1251), Ottokar II of Bohemia
0 @N6000000002187845515@ NOTE Otto IV, Count of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto IV, Count of Burgundy (1248-1302) was the son of Hugh de Chalon and Adelaide, Countess Palatine of Burgundy. By his mother, he was a grandson of Emperor Otto IV and Countess Beatrice II of Burgundy. By his father, he was desc
1 CONC ended from another branch of the Counts of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his father's death in 1266-7, he became Count of Chalons.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Philippa of Bar in 1371. The marriage was childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother, Adelaide, died on March 8, 1279, and Otto inherited the County. However, he was unable to secure real power in the County until 1295.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married his second wife, Mahaut of Artois, in 1285. They had three children: Jeanne, Blanche, and Robert. Of these, his daughters would marry eventual Kings of France, and both his son and elder daughter were to succeed him in 
1 CONC the County.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his death in 1302, his titles were inherited by his son.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Adelaide of Burgundy Count of Burgundy
1 CONT 1279–1302 Succeeded by
1 CONT Robert
0 @N6000000005598851881@ NOTE Otto consented to the marriage of Joan (Jeanne) of Burgundy to Prince Philip (later Philip V of France).
0 @N6000000002322315742@ NOTE Wenceslas brought Austria under his dynasty while using the influence of
1 CONT German colonists and craftsmen to keep Bohemia strong, prosperous, and
1 CONT culturally progressive, with a rich flowering of the arts, especially
1 CONT literature and architecture. He finally forced Austria to accept his son,
1 CONT Otakar II, as their duke in 1251.
0 @N6000000002187828352@ NOTE Wenceslaus I, King of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus I Premyslid (Czech Václav) (c. 1205 – September 23, 1253) was King of Bohemia from 1230 to 1253. Wenceslaus was the son of Ottokar I and Konstancia, daughter of Bela III, King of Hungary. He married Kunigunde of Hohenst
1 CONC aufen, daughter of Philip of Swabia, King of Germany. He encouraged large numbers of Germans to settle in the villages and towns in Bohemia and Moravia. As a sign of increasing development, courtesy of the new settlers, stone buil
1 CONC dings began to replace wooden ones in Prague.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1241 Wenceslaus successfully repelled a raid on Bohemia by Batu Khan, although Moravia suffered devastation at the hands of the Mongols. Wenceslaus' foreign policy was focused on uniting the Duchy of Austria with the Kingdom of
1 CONC  Bohemia following the death of the last Babenberg duke of Austria, Frederick II, during the battle of the Leitha river in 1246. Wenceslaus' eldest son, Vladislav, was married to Frederick's niece Gertrud and received the homage o
1 CONC f the Austrian barons as their future ruler, but died before he could be formally acclaimed as duke. Wenceslaus' second son Premysl Otakar then married Fredericks's sister Marguerite and claimed the duchy for himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Austrian question was put on hiatus when in 1249 Premysl Otakar led an uprising of nobles against his father. The rebellion was quelled, but Wenceslaus decided to have Otakar crowned as 'junior king' and to give him control of
1 CONC  Moravia. Wenceslaus died in 1253 and was succeeded by his son Premysl Ottokar II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Premysl Ottokar I King of Bohemia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Premysl Ottokar II 
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenceslaus_I%2C_King_of_Bohemia"
0 @N6000000002176663760@ NOTE Wenceslas brought Austria under his dynasty while using the influence of
1 CONT German colonists and craftsmen to keep Bohemia strong, prosperous, and
1 CONT culturally progressive, with a rich flowering of the arts, especially
1 CONT literature and architecture. He finally forced Austria to accept his son,
1 CONT Otakar II, as their duke in 1251.
