0 @N6000000003658751366@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT ARTHUR OF BRITTANY, Duke of Brittany, Count of Anjou, Earl of Richmond], son and heir, born 29 March 1187, at Nantes.  On his uncle, King Richard's death in 1199, he led a force into Anjou and Maine.  On Easter Day 1199 the barons
1 CONC  of Anjou, Touraine, and Maine acknowledged him as the rightful heir.  In 1202 he supported King Philippe Auguste of France against his uncle, King John.  IN July 1202 King Philippe knighted him at Gournay and betrothed him to the
1 CONC  king's infant daughter, Marie.  On 1 August 1202, when besieging his grandmother, Eleanor of Aquitaine, at Mirebeau, he was surprised and captured by John, who sent him to Falaise.  Early in 1203 John transferred him to Rouen, wh
1 CONC ere he disappeared before Easter.  He died probably about 3 April 1203, almost certainly at Rouen, bur possibly at Cherbourg, murdered either by John himself or at his command.  His body is alleged to have been bured at Nôtre Dame
1 CONC  des Prés.
0 @N6000000002187747440@ NOTE Philip I of Namur
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I (1175 – 9 October 1212), called the Noble, was the margrave of Namur from 1195 to his death. He was the second son of Baldwin V, Count of Hainault, and Margaret I, Countess of Flanders. His paternal grandmother was Alice,
1 CONC  Countess of Namur.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin V had fought a war with his uncle, Henry IV of Luxembourg, to establish Namur as independent from Luxembourg in 1190 and in 1194, the Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI declared Namur to be a margraviate. Baldwin's will left Namu
1 CONC r to Philip, but as a fief of Hainault. However, Theobald I of Bar, who had married Henry of Luxembourg's heiress, Ermesinda, refused to relinquish Namur and attacked Philip. The war lasted for three years until the Treaty of Dina
1 CONC nt, signed on 26 July 1199, recognised Philip as holder of Namur.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip was left as regent of Hainault while his elder brother Baldwin VI went on the Fourth Crusade and acted as guardian to the young heiresses Joanna and Margaret.
1 CONT
1 CONT During a war with France, Philip was subsequently imprisoned and only bought his freedom by marrying Marie, daughter of King Philip Augustus and Agnes of Merania, and sending his nieces as envoys to the royal court of France. This
1 CONC  insulted the barons of Flanders and Hainault and they revolted and forced him to give up the regency.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Namur, Philip's reign was a peaceful and pious promoter of social development. He intervened as the mediator between many feuding lords. He died of dysentery on 9 October 1212 in Valenciennes. He had designated his nephew Phili
1 CONC p of Courtenay as heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Baldwin I Margrave of Namur
1 CONT 1195–1212 Succeeded by
1 CONT Yolanda
0 @N6000000005598867556@ NOTE After the death of his brother Baldwin de Hainault VI in 1205, Philip became the guardian of Baldwin's daughters Margaret and Joan.  Philip was a liege vassel of Phillippe II of France.  He was styled as a wea k man, with interest
1 CONC s of his own.  At one time he promised not to marr y his nieces without the permission of Phillippe II.  In 1208, King Ph illippe demanded and received the direct wardship of the two girls.
0 @N6000000005598867544@ NOTE Philip met Richard I at Calais in December of 1189 on Richards return trip from England.  He accompanied Richard on to Normandie.  In March 1191, while in Sicily, Philip was able to mediate the differences betw een Tancred, Richar
1 CONC d I and Phillippe of France, which became known as the Treaty of Messina.
