0 @N6000000002176661769@ NOTE His younger brother, Louis, was made a ward of his and their mother when
1 CONT their father died in 1294. Being the younger brother, Louis had no claim to
1 CONT the crown at birth, but he was raised in his brother's court with cousins
1 CONT Frederick and Leopold of Lower Bavaia, from a line destined to extinction in
1 CONT 1340.
0 @N6000000002187825598@ NOTE Rudolf I, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf I of Bavaria (October 4, 1274, Basle – August 12, 1319), (German: Rudolf I , Herzog von Bayern, Pfalzgraf bei Rhein), since 1294 he was the Duke of Bavaria and Count Palatinate and a member of the Wittelsbach dynasty. Rudol
1 CONC f was the son of Louis II, Duke of Upper Bavaria, and Mechthild, a daughter of King Rudolph I.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 1294 and supported his father-in-law king Adolf of Nassau-Weilburg against his uncle, the Habsburg Albert of Austria. After Adolf's death Rudolf joined Albert's party but the strong dynastic policy of th
1 CONC e new king caused a new conflict. Since Albert put pressure on Rudolf he had to accept his younger brother Louis IV as co-regent in 1301. After Albert's assassination in 1308 Rudolf voted for Henry of Luxemburg and then accompanie
1 CONC d him to Italy. A civil war against his brother Louis IV due to new disputes on the partition of their lands was ended in 1313, when peace was made at Munich. Louis IV was elected German king in 1314 but Rudolf had voted for his o
1 CONC pponent Frederick of Austria. In 1317 after another long controversy with Louis IV he lost the Palatinate and it was agreed that Rudolf gave up his rule until the conflict of Louis with Habsburg was ended. But then Rudolf died alr
1 CONC eady in 1319.
1 CONT
1 CONT But by the Treaty of Pavia in 1329, Louis granted the Palatinate to Rudolf's sons Rudolf II "the Blind" and Rupert I and Rudolf's grandson Rupert II, a son of Adolf. This way finally Rudolf I and his son Rupert II became the ances
1 CONC tors of the older (Palatinate) line of the Wittelsbach dynasty, which returned to power also in Bavaria in 1777 after the extinction of the younger (Bavarian) line, the descendants of Louis IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married in Nuremberg 1 September 1294 with Mechtild of Nassau, daughter of king Adolf of Nassau-Weilburg and had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Ludwig (1297–before 5 April 1311).
1 CONT    2. Adolf, Count Palatine of the Rhine (27 September 1300, Wolfratshausen–29 January 1327).
1 CONT    3. Rudolf II "der Blinde" (8 August 1306, Wolfratshausen–4 October 1353, Neustadt).
1 CONT    4. Rupert I "der Rote" (9 June 1309, Wolfratshausen–16 February 1390).
1 CONT    5. Mathilde (1312–25 November 1375), married 1330/1 to Count John III of Sponheim.
1 CONT    6. Anna, died young.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Louis II Duke of Upper Bavaria
1 CONT 1294–1319 Succeeded by
1 CONT Louis IV
1 CONT Count Palatine of the Rhine
1 CONT 1294–1319 Succeeded by
1 CONT Adolf
